(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }

  var SSC_JE_EXTRA5 = {};
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.som = [
      P("Hooke's law holds only when the material is loaded", "within the elastic limit", "Hooke's law defines linear elastic behaviour", "Up to the proportional limit stress is proportional to strain (E = \u03C3/\u03B5); beyond yield the relation is non-linear and permanent set develops."),
      P("The ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain in a bar under uniaxial stress is called", "Poisson's ratio (m)", "Defined as -lateral/longitudinal strain", "Poisson's ratio m = -lateral strain / longitudinal strain. For most metals it lies between 0.25 and 0.35."),
      P("A bar 2 m long and 20 mm diameter is subjected to an axial pull of 20 kN. If E = 200 GPa, the elongation is", "0.64 mm", "Use dL = PL/(AE)", "A = \u03C0(20)2/4 = 314.16 mm2. dL = (20e3 x 2000)/(314.16 x 200e3) = 0.637 mm."),
      P("The maximum stress induced in a body due to suddenly applied load is", "twice the stress due to gradually applied load", "Dynamic factor for sudden loading is 2", "For a suddenly applied load the load is constant from zero, so the strain energy equals twice the work done, giving stress twice the static value."),
      P("Stress at a section rotating with the member is equal to", "P/A", "Direct normal stress formula", "Normal stress at a plane is the normal force divided by the area of that plane. For a section loaded centrally s = P/A."),
      P("A beam is said to be in pure bending when it is subjected to a constant", "bending moment with zero shear force", "No shear means no lateral variation of moment", "Pure bending has constant BM along the span (M = constant), so the shear force dM/dx = 0 throughout the region."),
      P("In a cantilever the maximum bending moment occurs at the", "fixed support", "Moment is maximum where shear is zero or at support", "For a cantilever with a point load at the free end, M = WL at the fixed end, which is the maximum."),
      P("Modulus of resilience is", "strain energy stored per unit volume up to the elastic limit", "Energy absorbed without permanent deformation", "It equals (yield stress)2/(2E), the area under the elastic portion of the stress-strain curve, and represents energy the material absorbs without permanent set."),
      P("Modulus of toughness is the area under the", "complete stress-strain curve up to fracture", "Total energy absorbed before rupture", "Toughness = \u222B \u03C3 d\u03B5 from zero strain to fracture strain; it measures total energy absorbed per unit volume including plastic work."),
      P("For a thin cylindrical shell under internal pressure p, the circumferential (hoop) stress is", "pD/(2t)", "Hoop stress uses diameter", "Circumferential stress = pD/(2t). Longitudinal stress = pD/(4t). Hence hoop stress is twice the longitudinal stress."),
      P("For a thin spherical shell under internal pressure p, the stress in the wall is", "pD/(4t)", "Spherical shells have equal biaxial stress", "Hoop and meridional stresses are equal = pD/(4t) for a thin sphere, both about half of a cylinder's hoop stress."),
      P("The maximum shear stress in a simply supported beam of rectangular section occurs at", "the neutral axis", "Shear stress is parabolic, peak at centre", "Shear stress \u03C4 = 1.5 V/A at the neutral axis for a rectangle, zero at top and bottom fibres."),
      P("The deflection of a simply supported beam of span L carrying a central point load W is", "WL\u00B3/(48EI)", "Standard central load case", "Central load: \u03B4 = WL\u00B3/(48EI). Uniformly distributed load: 5wL\u2074/(384EI). Cantilever end load: WL\u00B3/(3EI)."),
      P("Shaft is said to be in pure torsion when it carries", "only torque, no bending moment", "Torsion-only loading", "Pure torsion imposes only shear stress \u03C4 = Tr/J; bending adds normal stress from M/Z."),
      P("The polar moment of inertia of a solid circular shaft of diameter d is", "\u03C0d\u2074/32", "J = (Ip) about longitudinal axis", "Polar second moment of area J = \u03C0 d\u2074/32 for a solid circle. The angle of twist \u03B8 = TL/(GJ)."),
      P("For equal strength in torsion and bending, the equivalent bending moment for a shaft carrying torque T and moment M is", "0.5[M + \u221A(M\u00B2 + T\u00B2)]", "Max principal stress theory equivalent", "According to the maximum principal stress theory, equivalent bending moment Me = 0.5[M + \u221A(M\u00B2+T\u00B2)] and equivalent torque Te = \u221A(M\u00B2+T\u00B2)."),
      P("The ratio of maximum shear stress to average shear stress in a solid circular shaft is", "4/3", "Solid circle shear distribution", "Shear stress is linear across the radius; for a solid circular section \u03C4max/\u03C4avg = 4/3. For a hollow circle \u03C4max/\u03C4avg = 2[\u221A(1+n\u00B2+n\u2074)]/(3(1+n\u00B2))."),
      P("The neutral axis of a beam coincides with the centroidal axis when the section is", "subjected to pure bending of a homogeneous symmetric section", "NA passes through centroid when axial force is zero", "In pure bending of a homogeneous elastic beam, the neutral axis passes through the centroid of the cross-section; it coincides with the centroidal axis."),
      P("A rivet joint in which the plates are overlapped is a", "lap joint", "Plates overlap, single-shear loading", "In a lap joint one plate overlaps the other, the rivets are loaded in single shear, and the bearing is on one plate thickness."),
      P("Longitudinal (butt) joints are used to join the plates which form the", "shell walls of a pressure vessel, parallel to the axis", "Join along the length of the shell", "In a cylindrical boiler shell, the longitudinal joint joins the plates along the axis and sees the hoop stress; circumferential joints join shells end to end."),
      P("The caulking and fullering of riveted joints is done to", "make the joint leak-proof", "Seal against fluid leakage", "Caulking (hammering the free edge) and fullering (using a fuller tool) displace the plate edge tightly against the plate/rivet to stop leakage under pressure."),
      P("A beam with the same moment of inertia but twice the elastic modulus deflects", "half as much", "Deflection is inversely proportional to EI", "Since \u03B4 \u221D 1/(EI), doubling E halves the deflection for the same load, span and section."),
      P("The bulk modulus K, modulus of rigidity G and Young's modulus E of a material are related by", "E = 9KG/(3K + G)", "Combine E = 3K(1-2m) with E = 2G(1+m)", "Eliminating Poisson's ratio from E = 3K(1-2m) and E = 2G(1+m) gives E = 9KG/(3K+G)."),
      P("Thermal stress is induced in a bar", "when its free expansion is restrained", "Restrained expansion creates stress", "If a bar's thermal expansion is fully or partially prevented, an axial stress s = E \u03B1 \u0394T develops; a free bar expands with zero stress."),
      P("Bearing stress in a riveted joint is the", "crushing stress on plate/rivet contact area", "Localised contact pressure", "Bearing (crushing) stress = P/(d x t) where d is the rivet diameter and t the plate thickness, acting over the projected contact area."),
      P("In a cotter joint the tensile load is transmitted through", "the cotter and socket on one side, spigot on the other", "Cotter is a tapered wedge", "The cotter, driven transversely through slots, locks spigot and socket so tension passes through both members; it fails in double shear bearing and tension."),
      P("The stresses developed at the outer fibre of a curved beam in tension are", "more than predicted by the straight-beam formula", "Stress straddles the neutral axis, which shifts inward", "For a curved beam the neutral axis shifts toward the centre of curvature, so inner-fibre stress increases beyond the straight-beam (M/Z) value and outer-fibre stress drops."),
      P("Euler's crippling load for a column with both ends fixed is", "4\u03C0\u00B2EI/L\u00B2", "Effective length = L/2", "Crippling load PE = \u03C0\u00B2EI/(Le)\u00B2. Both ends fixed: Le = L/2, giving 4\u03C0\u00B2EI/L\u00B2. Both hinged: Le = L."),
      P("The maximum compressive stress in a column of circular section under eccentric load acts", "at the edge nearest the line of the load", "Direct plus bending add on the near side", "On the side of the load, direct stress and bending-stress compression add; on the far side they subtract, so tension appears only if the eccentricity is large."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.tom = [
      P("A kinematic pair with two sliding elements is a", "sliding pair", "Two links slide relative", "A sliding pair allows relative translational (sliding) motion between two links, e.g. piston and cylinder, with a single DOF."),
      P("In a four-bar chain the simplest link is the ground link and the two links in contact with it are the", "cranks (or rockers)", "Links attached to the frame", "The fixed link is the frame; the two links pivoted to it are cranks if they rotate fully or rockers/levers if they oscillate; the fourth is the coupler."),
      P("Grashof's criterion for a four-bar mechanism to give continuous rotation of at least one crank is", "s + l \u2264 p + q", "s shortest, l longest, p and q the other two", "In a four-bar linkage, at least one link makes a full revolution if the sum of the shortest and longest links is less than or equal to the sum of the other two."),
      P("The instantaneous centre of links 1 and 3 in a four-bar chain having centres 12, 23, 34, 41, 13, 24 is found at the", "intersection of the centres joining lines", "Kennedy's theorem", "By Kennedy's theorem three instantaneous centres of three links lie on a straight line; centre 13 is at the intersection of the lines extended through 14-34 and 12-23."),
      P("A round-and-flat link combined to give turning motion forms a", "turning pair", "Rotation relative motion", "When two links are connected so one turns relative to the other about a fixed axis (pin or bearing), the pair is a turning (revolute) pair with a single rotational DOF."),
      P("Pantograph mechanism is used for", "reproducing drawings to a chosen scale", "Parallel-link copying", "The pantograph is a parallel-link mechanism based on similar triangles that reproduces, enlarges or reduces a traced path with a fixed scale ratio."),
      P("The value of L/D for Idler's apparatus to generate a cycloidal path with a crank of length r is approximately", "L/D = 2", "Poncelet's linkage geometry", "For Poncelet's/the idler linkage, a L/D ratio of 2 makes the traced point follow a cycloid with crank length roughly r = L/3."),
      P("The Swiss (Geneva) mechanism converts", "continuous rotation into intermittent rotation", "Star-wheel indexing", "The Geneva drive has a driving pin rotating continuously while the star wheel dwells between indexed steps, producing intermittent motion with locked dwell."),
      P("Law of gearing states that the common normal at the point of contact divides the line of centres", "in the inverse ratio of the angular velocities", "Condition for constant velocity ratio", "The common normal at contact passes through the pitch point, which divides the line of centres proportionally to the angular velocities: r1/r2 = \u03C92/\u03C91, ensuring constant velocity ratio."),
      P("In involute gearing the pressure angle is the angle between the", "line of action and the common tangent to pitch circles", "Path of contact geometry", "The pressure angle is measured between the line of action (common normal) and the tangent to the pitch circles at the pitch point; typical values are 14.5\u00B0 and 20\u00B0."),
      P("Cycloidal teeth are generated by rolling a circle on a straight line; the curve generated is a", "cycloid", "Rolling circle on a line", "A point on a circle rolling along a straight line traces a cycloid. Rolling inside a circle gives a hypocycloid; outside gives an epicycloid."),
      P("The number of teeth on two meshing gears is 20 and 40 with module 5 mm. The centre distance is", "150 mm", "a = m(z1 + z2)/2", "Centre distance a = m(z1+z2)/2 = 5(20+40)/2 = 150 mm. Pitch circle radius r = mz/2."),
      P("The interference in involute gears can be avoided by", "increasing the pressure angle or using larger pinion corrected teeth", "Under-cutting avoidance", "Interference (contact outside the involute addendum circle) is avoided by increasing the pressure angle, using a larger pinion, profile shifting, or reducing addendum."),
      P("For a tuning fork to vibrate, the effective length of each prong is", "0.242 times its length", "Free-free bar correction", "The vibrating length of a tuning fork prong is about 0.242 L, slightly less than the actual length because the bend at the stem acts partly as a mass."),
      P("A device that allows rotation in one direction only is a", "ratchet and pawl", "Unidirectional clutch", "A ratchet wheel with a spring-loaded pawl transmits torque in one direction and locks against reverse motion; the wrap angle is small to reduce wear."),
      P("A universal (Hooke's) joint transmits constant angular velocity ratio only when", "the driving and driven shafts are parallel to the intermediate shaft with equal joint angles at 90\u00B0 phasing", "Two joints in phase", "Two universal joints spaced with the intermediate shaft give constant velocity when both joint angles are equal and the yokes are kept in phase (90\u00B0 orientation)."),
      P("In static balancing of a rigid rotor the condition to be satisfied is", "\u03A3mr = 0", "Vector sum of centrifugal forces zero", "Static balance requires the resultant of all m.r.e products to be zero so the rotor is in equilibrium without rotation; dynamic balance additionally requires \u03A3m.r.l.e = 0."),
      P("The flywheel controls", "speed fluctuations during a cycle", "Momentary surge, not the mean speed", "A flywheel stores/releases energy to smooth cyclic speed variation; the governor, by contrast, regulates the mean speed as the load changes."),
      P("The maximum fluctuation of energy in a flywheel is proportional to", "the square of the coefficient of speed fluctuation times total energy", "K.E. relationship", "Max fluctuation of energy DE = m.R\u00B2.\u03C9\u00B2.Cs = I.\u03C9\u00B2.Cs where Cs = (Nmax - Nmin)/Nmean is the coefficient of fluctuation of speed."),
      P("A body vibrating with damping in which it returns to rest without oscillating follows", "critically damped motion", "Damping ratio = 1", "At critical damping (c = 2\u221A(km)) the system returns to equilibrium in the shortest time without oscillation; below it underdamped (oscillatory), above it overdamped."),
      P("For a vibrating system the logarithmic decrement is defined as", "the natural log of the ratio of two successive amplitudes", "Amplitude decay measure", "Logarithmic decrement \u03B4 = ln(x1/x2) = (2\u03C0\u03BE)/\u221A(1-\u03BE\u00B2), where x1 and x2 are successive amplitudes and \u03BE is the damping ratio."),
      P("The whirling speed of a shaft is the speed at which", "the shaft becomes dynamically unstable and bows laterally", "Resonance of lateral vibration", "Whirling (critical) speed coincides with the natural transverse frequency; at that speed even small unbalance builds up large lateral deflections."),
      P("The gyroscopic couple applied to bearings by a spinning disc precessing about a perpendicular axis is", "I.\u03C9.\u03C9p", "Product of inertia and spin velocity times precession", "Gyroscopic couple C = I.\u03C9.\u03C9p where I is polar moment of inertia of the disc, \u03C9 the spin velocity and \u03C9p the precession angular velocity."),
      P("In a reciprocating engine of mass m, crank radius r and angular velocity \u03C9, the primary unbalanced force is", "m.\u03C9\u00B2.r.cos\u03B8", "Primary = m r \u03C9\u00B2 cos\u03B8", "The inertia force from the piston resolves into m.\u03C9\u00B2.r.cos\u03B8 (primary, frequency = crank speed) and m.\u03C9\u00B2.r.cos2\u03B8/n (secondary)."),
      P("The distance between the centres of two meshing gears joined by an idler is a", "negative (back) quantity when compared with direct mesh", "Idler only reverses rotation", "An idler gear transmits motion between two gears without changing the velocity ratio; the centre distance is simply the sum of the pitch radii with the idler between them."),
      P("In a cam-follower system the follower dwells while the", "cam profile has a circular arc of constant radius", "Dwell = no follower motion", "During the dwell the follower stays still though the cam rotates; produced by a circular (constant radius) portion of the cam profile centred on the cam axis."),
      P("The displacement of a follower moving uniformly is represented by a", "straight line on the displacement diagram", "Constant slope", "Uniform motion gives linear displacement-time (constant slope); uniform acceleration gives a parabola; simple harmonic motion gives a sine curve."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.thermo = [
      P("Zeroth law of thermodynamics provides the basis for", "temperature measurement", "Thermal equilibrium transitivity", "Zeroth law states that if two bodies are each in thermal equilibrium with a third, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other - the foundation of temperature definition."),
      P("The first law of thermodynamics is a statement of the conservation of", "energy", "Energy cannot be created or destroyed", "The first law dQ = dU + dW expresses energy conservation for a closed system; for a cycle the net heat equals the net work."),
      P("Entropy of a reversible adiabatic process", "remains constant", "ds = dQ/T = 0 for reversible adiabatic", "A reversible adiabatic (isentropic) process has constant entropy because reversible means no heat transfer and adiabatic gives dQ = 0, hence ds = 0."),
      P("During a throttling process the", "enthalpy remains constant", "h = u + pv unchanging", "Throttling is a constant-enthalpy irreversible flow process used to measure wetness fraction in steam calorimeters; temperature and entropy change according to the fluid's Joule-Thomson coefficient."),
      P("An ideal Otto cycle consists of", "two isentropics and two constant-volume (isochoric) processes", "Four stages", "The Otto cycle: isentropic compression, constant-volume heat addition, isentropic expansion, constant-volume heat rejection. Its efficiency depends on the compression ratio r: \u03B7 = 1-1/r^(\u03B3-1)."),
      P("The efficiency of an ideal Carnot engine operating between 800 K and 300 K is", "62.5%", "\u03B7 = 1 - T2/T1", "Carnot efficiency = 1 - T2/T1 = 1 - 300/800 = 0.625 = 62.5%. This is the maximum possible efficiency between the two reservoirs."),
      P("Clausius statement of the second law concerns the impossibility of", "heat flowing from cold to hot without external work", "Heat pump limitation", "Clausius: it is impossible for a cyclic device to transfer heat from a cold body to a hot body without the expenditure of work."),
      P("Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law concerns the impossibility of a heat engine with", "a single reservoir producing net work", "Perpetual motion machine of the second kind", "Kelvin-Planck: no cyclic engine can convert all the heat it receives from a single reservoir into work; some heat must be rejected to a low-temperature sink."),
      P("The efficiency of a Rankine cycle increases when", "the boiler pressure is raised and condenser pressure is lowered", "Greater temperature range", "Raising boiler pressure raises the mean temperature of heat addition and lowering condenser pressure lowers the rejection temperature, both increasing the cycle efficiency."),
      P("The dryness fraction x of wet steam is measured with", "a throttling calorimeter", "Constant enthalpy sampling", "A throttling calorimeter throttles wet steam to superheat and measures the temperature; from the superheat enthalpy the initial dryness fraction is computed by energy balance."),
      P("Fourier's law of conduction states that the rate of heat flow is", "proportional to the temperature gradient and the area", "dQ/dt = -kA dT/dx", "Conduction rate Qdot = -kA(dT/dx). The minus sign shows heat flows down the temperature gradient; k is the thermal conductivity."),
      P("For one-dimensional steady conduction through a plane wall, the temperature profile is", "linear", "Constant k, linear gradient", "With constant thermal conductivity the gradient dT/dx is constant, so temperature varies linearly across the wall; in a cylinder the profile is logarithmic."),
      P("Finned surfaces are added to a heat-transfer surface to", "increase the heat-transfer area for a given size", "Enhance convection area", "Fins/radiators increase the convection area without adding much material; they help when the air-side film coefficient is low."),
      P("The effectiveness of a counter-flow heat exchanger is", "higher than that of a parallel-flow exchanger for the same conditions", "Better LMTD arrangement", "Counter-flow gives a higher LMTD than parallel flow for the same inlet/outlet temperatures, so a smaller exchanger achieves the same duty."),
      P("LMTD correction factor F is always", "less than unity", "Account for cross/multi-pass geometry", "For heat exchangers of other than pure counter-flow or parallel-flow, the true mean temperature difference is F x LMTD with F \u2264 1."),
      P("Steam enters a nozzle with negligible velocity. The exit velocity is given by", "c2 = \u221A(2(h1 - h2))", "Energy equation for a nozzle", "For an adiabatic nozzle with negligible inlet velocity, kinetic energy at exit equals the enthalpy drop: c2 = \u221A(2(h1-h2)); h in J/kg."),
      P("The maximum mass flow of steam through a nozzle occurs when the pressure ratio is the", "critical pressure ratio", "Flow is choked", "At the critical pressure ratio pc/p0 (about 0.577 for steam) the flow velocity reaches sonic and mass flow is a maximum; beyond that the nozzle diverges to accelerate the flow."),
      P("A condenser in a steam plant maintains", "a low back pressure to increase work output", "Vacuum at turbine exhaust", "The condenser creates a vacuum (low turbine back pressure), increasing the Rankine work output per unit steam and raising cycle efficiency."),
      P("In a vapour-compression refrigeration cycle the refrigerant at the compressor inlet is", "a saturated or slightly superheated vapour", "Low pressure vapour", "The compressor takes low-pressure vapour (saturated or slightly superheated) and compresses it; after the condenser it is a high-pressure liquid before throttling."),
      P("The COP of a refrigerator operating between T1 (cold) and T2 (hot) is", "T1/(T2 - T1)", "COP_ref = TL/(TH - TL)", "COP_ref = QL/W = TL/(TH - TL) for the ideal reversed Carnot cycle. The heat pump COP = TH/(TH - TL) = COP_ref + 1."),
      P("A closed thermodynamic system", "exchanges energy but not mass with the surroundings", "Control mass", "A closed system (control mass) allows heat and work transfer across its boundary, but no mass crosses the boundary."),
      P("For an adiabatic process of an ideal gas the relation between p and v is", "pv^\u03B3 = constant", "abla \u03B3 = cp/cv", "For a reversible adiabatic (isentropic) ideal-gas process pv^\u03B3 = const. Combined with the gas law also T v^(\u03B3-1) = const."),
      P("In a polytropic process pv^n = constant, the specific heat is", "cv(n-\u03B3)/(n-1)", "Derived from first law for a polytrope", "The polytropic specific heat cn = cv(\u03B3-n)/(1-n) or equivalently cv(n-\u03B3)/(n-1); for n = \u03B3 it reduces to zero (adiabatic) and for n = 1 it is cp."),
      P("Entropy increases for real (irreversible) thermodynamic processes because of", "internal irreversibilities generating entropy", "Second law", "In any real process, entropy production from friction, unrestrained expansion and finite-temperature transfer adds entropy above the reversible value; ds > dQ/T."),
      P("The critical point of water occurs at", "221.2 bar, 374.15\u00B0C", "Water's critical state", "The critical point of water is 221.2 bar and 374.15\u00B0C (647 K)); above it no amount of pressure liquefies vapour - only a single supercritical phase exists."),
      P("Refrigerant R-134a used widely in car air conditioners has the chemical name", "1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane", "HFC refrigerant", "R-134a is C2H2F4 (CH2FCF3), a non-ozone-depleting HFC with near-zero ODP, used to replace R-12 in automotive AC systems."),
      P("The value of the characteristic gas constant R for air is", "0.287 kJ/kgK", "R = cp - cv = 1.005 - 0.718", "For air R = cp - cv = 0.287 kJ/kgK. Universal gas constant Ru = 8.314 kJ/kmolK divided by molecular weight 28.97."),
      P("Boyle's law holds at constant temperature and states that", "pressure is inversely proportional to volume", "Isothermal ideal gas law", "At constant temperature pv = constant for an ideal gas - doubling pressure halves the volume, known as Boyle's law."),
      P("In a gas turbine using the Brayton cycle, the compression and expansion processes are", "isentropic and constant-pressure heat addition/rejection", "Standard air cycle", "The Brayton cycle comprises isentropic compression, constant-pressure heat addition, isentropic expansion and constant-pressure heat rejection - the ideal model of the simple gas turbine."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.fluids = [
      P("Specific weight of water at standard conditions is approximately", "9810 N/m\u00B3", "Density x g", "Specific weight = \u03C1g = 1000 kg/m\u00B3 x 9.81 m/s\u00B2 = 9810 N/m\u00B3. Specific gravity is the ratio to water's properties."),
      P("Pressure at a depth h below the free surface of a liquid of density \u03C1 is", "\u03C1gh pascal", "Hydrostatic pressure", "Pressure p = \u03C1gh measured below the free surface, independent of vessel shape; it is a normal stress acting equally in all directions."),
      P("A piezometer measures", "pressure at a point in a liquid only", "Simple manometer for liquids", "A piezometer is a vertical tube open to atmosphere attached to the vessel; it measures gauge pressure at the point, applicable only to liquids (not gases)."),
      P("The pressure difference measured by a U-tube manometer with heavy liquid of SG S is", "h(S \u2212 S1)g \u03C1w for liquid lines", "Balanced head technique", "Using the same liquid branch, p1 - p2 = Z(S - S1)\u03C1wg, where S and S1 are the specific gravities of the manometric and flowing liquids respectively."),
      P("A body floats in a liquid when its weight equals the", "weight of the displaced liquid", "Archimedes' principle", "For flotation, the buoyant force (weight of displaced fluid) equals the body's weight; the line of action must pass through the centre of buoyancy."),
      P("The metacentric height GM of a floating body is", "the distance between the metacentre M and the centre of gravity G", "Stability criterion", "GM = MB - GB (or BM - BG). Positive GM means the body is stable; negative GM means unstable (overturns)."),
      P("For a floating body, the value of GM for stable equilibrium is", "positive", "GM > 0 is stable", "Stable equilibrium of a floating body requires the metacentre to lie above the centre of gravity, i.e. a positive metacentric height."),
      P("The centre of pressure of a plane vertical submerged surface is located", "below the centroid of the surface", "Ycp = I/(A.Y) + Y of centroid", "Centre of pressure lies below the centroid by distance = IG/(A.y) from the free surface, since pressure increases with depth."),
      P("Reynolds number is the ratio of", "inertia force to viscous force", "Flow regime indicator", "Re = \u03C1VD/\u03BC = VD/\u03BD. For pipe flow Re < 2000 laminar, Re > 4000 turbulent, between is transitional."),
      P("The streamlines, streaklines and pathlines of fluid motion coincide when flow is", "steady", "Instantaneous alignment", "In steady flow, velocity at a point is constant with time, so streamlines, streaklines and pathlines all coincide."),
      P("Euler's equation of motion in fluid flow is", "dp/\u03C1 + g dz + v dv = 0", "Derivative along a streamline", "Euler's equation dp/\u03C1 + gdz + vdv = 0 assumes inviscid flow along a streamline; integrating with constant density yields Bernoulli's equation."),
      P("A venturimeter measures flow by creating a", "throat where velocity rises and pressure falls", "Converging-diverging meter", "The throat of a venturimeter contracts the flow, raising velocity and lowering pressure by Bernoulli; the pressure difference gives the flow rate through the discharge coefficient."),
      P("In a venturimeter the discharge coefficient Cd is always", "less than 1", "Losses and head recovery", "Cd accounts for throat losses and the difference between actual and theoretical flow (owing to friction), and is typically about 0.95-0.99."),
      P("The velocity of flow measured by a pitot tube is", "V = \u221A(2gh)", "Stagnation-pressure formula", "The pitot tube converts kinetic energy to pressure head at the stagnation point; V = \u221A(2gh) using the difference between total and static head, with a coefficient below unity."),
      P("The equation of continuity for steady incompressible flow states", "A1V1 = A2V2", "Mass conservation", "For incompressible steady flow, volume flow Q = AV remains constant along the stream: A1V1 = A2V2, so velocity rises as area falls."),
      P("The velocity gradient in a fluid at a solid boundary is", "maximum close to the wall", "No-slip condition", "At the wall the fluid sticks (zero velocity); the gradient dV/dy is largest near the wall and zero at the centre of the section - giving maximum shear stress at the wall."),
      P("Newton's law of viscosity states that shear stress is", "proportional to the velocity gradient", "\u03C4 = \u03BC.dV/dy", "\u03C4 = \u03BC(dV/dy) for Newtonian fluids. Kinematic viscosity \u03BD = \u03BC/\u03C1 with SI unit m\u00B2/s (stoke = cm\u00B2/s)."),
      P("For fully developed laminar flow in a circular pipe, the velocity profile is", "parabolic", "Poiseuille flow", "The laminar pipe velocity profile is paraboloid: V = Vmax(1 - (r/R)\u00B2) with Vmax = 2 Vavg. Friction factor f = 64/Re."),
      P("The head loss in a pipe of length L carrying velocity V with friction factor f is", "fLV\u00B2/(2gD)", "Darcy-Weisbach equation", "Darcy-Weisbach: hf = f L V\u00B2/(2 g D). For laminar flow f = 64/Re; for turbulent flow f comes from the Moody chart/Colebrook equation."),
      P("The kinetic energy correction factor for fully developed laminar pipe flow is", "2.0", "\u03B1 = V\u00B3 average over Vavg\u00B3", "The kinetic-energy correction factor \u03B1 = 2 for laminar parabolic flow, while the momentum correction factor \u03B2 = 4/3. For turbulent flow both are near 1."),
      P("A hydraulic jump occurs when", "supercritical flow changes to subcritical flow", "Energy dissipation", "A hydraulic jump converts supercritical (Froude > 1) flow to subcritical (Froude < 1) with violent turbulence; it is used to dissipate energy below spillways."),
      P("In open-channel flow the Froude number determines", "whether the flow is critical, subcritical or supercritical", "Gravity vs inertia", "Fr = V/\u221A(gD). Fr = 1 critical, < 1 tranquil/subcritical, > 1 rapid/supercritical. At critical depth specific energy is a minimum."),
      P("The most economical trapezoidal channel section has", "its hydraulic radius equal to half the depth", "Best hydraulic section", "For maximum discharge at a given area, the best trapezoidal section has the hydraulic radius R = y/2 and the side slopes at 60\u00B0 (half-hexagon)."),
      P("For a thin rectangular weir the discharge is proportional to", "H^(3/2)", "Weir formula", "Rectangular weir Q = (2/3)Cd \u221A(2g) L H^(3/2); a triangular (V-notch) weir gives Q \u221D H^(5/2)."),
      P("The critical depth in a rectangular channel of specific energy E is", "2E/3", "Minimum energy at critical depth", "At critical flow for a rectangular channel, the critical depth yc = 2E/3 and the critical velocity Vc = \u221A(g.yc)."),
      P("A floating body with its centre of buoyancy above the centre of gravity is in", "stable equilibrium", "Righting moment positive", "If the centre of buoyancy is above the centre of gravity, any tilt produces a restoring moment, so the equilibrium is always stable."),
      P("Capillary rise in a small tube is inversely proportional to the tube", "diameter", "Surface tension balance", "Capillary rise h = 4\u03C3cos\u03B8/(\u03C1gd); for water in clean glass it is about 30/d (mm, d in mm). Smaller tubes give larger rise."),
      P("Dimensional analysis result for pipe flow from Buckingham's \u03C0-theorem requires", "dimensionless groups with the diameter, viscosity and density", "Three primary dimensions", "For pipe flow variables (D, V, \u03C1, \u03BC, L, \u03B5), dimensional analysis gives the \u03C0-groups Re, L/D and \u03B5/D, which form the basis of the Moody chart."),
      P("A turbine develops 400 kW under a head of 50 m with unit speed 60 rpm. The speed under a 100 m head by turbine affinity is", "85 rpm approximately", "N \u221D \u221AH", "Unit speed N1 = N/\u221AH; therefore N = N1\u221AH. Scaling to the double head: N2 = 60 x \u221A(100/50) = 84.85 rpm."),
      P("The pipe line conveying water to a turbine is laid along a grade that is", "slightly above the hydraulic gradient to avoid air locks", "Air-release at summit", "Pipelines should run just under the hydraulic gradient so summits remain at positive pressure and air pockets (which cause head loss) cannot collect."),
      P("Water hammer in a pipeline occurs when the flow is", "suddenly stopped or reversed by a valve", "Sudden closure", "A sudden valve closure converts flow kinetic energy to a pressure surge wave travelling at aE c = \u221A(K/\u03C1)/(1+KD/E t); extra pressure \u0394p = \u03C1cV."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.design = [
      P("A shaft transmitting power P kW at N rpm carries a torque", "T = 60P/(2\u03C0N) kN m", "Power-torque relation", "T = 60P/(2\u03C0N) N m for P in watts, or T = (P x 9.55 x 10\u2076)/N N-mm. Then d = (16T/\u03C4)\u00B9/\u00B3 for shear stress \u03C4."),
      P("A solid shaft of diameter d has strength in torsional bending proportional to", "d\u00B3", "Section modulus J/(d/2)", "The polar section modulus Zp = \u03C0d\u00B3/16, so strength (allowable surface stress torque) varies with d\u00B3, while stiffness (angle of twist) varies with d\u2074."),
      P("In a belt drive at the point of slipping, the tension ratio follows", "T1/T2 = e^(\u03BC\u03B8)", "Belt friction equation", "T1/T2 = e^(\u03BC\u03B8), where \u03B8 is the wrap angle; the power transmitted = (T1 - T2)V. Centrifugal tension reduces the effective pull."),
      P("For maximum power transmission by a belt the belt speed should be", "V = \u221A(Tm/3m)", "Derivative of power with speed", "Power P = (T1 - T2)V is maximised when the centrifugal tension Tc = m V\u00B2 = Tm/3; optimum speed V = \u221A(Tm/(3m))."),
      P("The initial tension in a belt drive is", "half the sum of tight and slack side tensions", "Static pre-tension", "With the belt stationary, initial tension T0 = (T1 + T2)/2, assuming no slip, relating the working tensions to the pre-set movement."),
      P("In a rope drive the maximum stress in the rope is the sum of", "direct stress, bending stress and centrifugal stress", "Combined loading", "Total stress = T/(n.A) + Eb(d/D) + \u03C1V\u00B2; bending stress uses the rope diameter to sheave diameter ratio and the rope's modulus."),
      P("A flat-belt drive transmits more power with a", "smaller angle of contact enhanced by an idler pulley", "Wrap angle increase", "Using a jockey (idler) pulley on the slack side increases the angle of wrap, raising e^(\u03BC\u03B8) and so the transmitted power."),
      P("The function of a spur gear key is to", "transmit torque between shaft and gear hub", "Positive torque coupling", "A parallel key (square or rectangular) fits in keyways of shaft and hub and resists the turning moment through the shearing/bearing stresses."),
      P("The effective length of a key in terms of torque capacity is governed by", "shear and crushing failure of the key", "Design limits", "A key of length l, width b and height t: shearing strength T = dbl\u03C4/2 and crushing T = (dt/2)l\u03C3c. The key must satisfy both."),
      P("In a coupling the driving torque is transmitted by", "shear in bolts and keys", "Torque path", "A flange coupling transfers torque through the key to the flange and then through the bolts in shear; the required bolt circle diameter is obtained by T = (D/2) n (\u03C0d\u00B2/4)\u03C4."),
      P("The main purpose of a flywheel is to", "limit the fluctuation of speed caused by cyclic torque variations", "Absorb energy peaks", "The flywheel stores kinetic energy during the excess-torque portion of the cycle and releases it during deficiency, reducing the coefficient of speed fluctuation."),
      P("The standard hardness scales in design for gears and screws are", "Brinell, Rockwell and Vickers", "Indentation hardness", "Common hardness tests are Brinell (ball), Rockwell (cone/ball with depth measurement) and Vickers (pyramidal diamond); they correlate with tensile strength for steels."),
      P("Fatigue failure in machine elements is characterised by", "progressive cracking under repeated stress below the static yield", "Cyclic loading", "Fatigue cracks initiate at stress raisers and propagate under repeated stress cycles even when peak stress is below the yield strength, ending in sudden failure."),
      P("Endurance limit of a steel for completely reversed bending is approximately", "0.5 Sult", "Half the tensile strength", "For steels the endurance limit Se \u2248 0.5 Sut; several modifying factors (size, surface, reliability, temperature, stress-concentration) reduce the design value."),
      P("The Goodman line criterion for fluctuating stress is", "(Sa/S'e) + (Sm/Sut) = 1", "Mean + amplitude criterion", "The Goodman line combines the amplitude stress Sa and mean stress Sm: Sa/S'e + Sm/Sut = 1, giving the safe amplitude for a given mean stress."),
      P("The Soderberg criterion uses the", "yield strength for the mean-stress axis", "Conservative failure line", "Soderberg line Sa/S'e + Sm/Sy = 1 is more conservative than Goodman since it allows for yield of the mean-stress component."),
      P("Endurance strength is reduced by the presence of a", "notch (stress raiser)", "Kf factor", "Notches and holes localise stress; the fatigue stress-concentration factor Kf = 1 + q(Kt - 1) where q is the notch sensitivity, degrading the endurance limit."),
      P("The stress concentration factor Kt for a hole in a wide plate is", "3", "Classic geometric factor", "For an infinite plate with a circular hole, Kt = 3 under uniaxial tension, independent of hole size (based on net section stress)."),
      P("In a bolted joint under fluctuating loads, the bolt is preloaded so that", "the joint does not separate and the bolt fatigue load is small", "Stiffness ratio effect", "Pre-load makes the members carry most of the external load (by the stiffness ratio), minimising the bolt's amplitude stress and the risk of fatigue and loosening."),
      P("The length of engagement required to prevent stripping of a screw thread is based on", "bearing (crushing) pressure between thread surfaces", "Thread shear and bearing", "For a nut, the stress that sets required engagement length is the bearing pressure on the projected thread area \u03C0.d.p.n or thread shear, whichever governs."),
      P("A power screw of diameter d, mean diameter dm and lead l has the torque to raise a load given by", "T = (W dm/2) tan(\u03B1 + \u03C6)", "Screw helix angle", "Torque to raise: T = (W dm/2) tan(\u03B1 + \u03C6), where tan\u03B1 = l/(\u03C0dm) and tan\u03C6 = \u03BC. Lowering uses tan(\u03C6 - \u03B1); self-locking when \u03C6 > \u03B1."),
      P("The advantage of a double-start square thread over single-start for a given pitch is", "finer lead for the same helix angle movement, giving quicker motion with same helix angle", "Twice the lead", "A double-start thread has twice the lead per revolution at the same pitch, giving faster traverse without reducing thread flank strength."),
      P("Spring index is defined as the ratio of", "mean coil diameter to wire diameter", "C = D/d", "Spring index C = D/d. Wahl correction factor K adds curvature/concentration effects to the stress formula \u03C4 = K(8FD/(\u03C0d\u00B3))."),
      P("For helical springs, the deflection of a spring is proportional to", "the square of the number of active coils times the force", "Stiffness relation", "Deflection \u03B4 = 8FD\u00B3n/(Gd\u2074), so stiffness k = Gd\u2074/(8D\u00B3n) - stiffer with more coils in the denominator."),
      P("Leaf springs are designed on the basis of", "bending stress and deflection under the applied load", "Cantilever beam model", "A leaf (semi-elliptic) spring is analysed like a cantilever beam; bending stress \u03C3 = 6FL/(nbt\u00B2) and deflection \u03B4 = 6FL\u00B3/(Enbt\u00B3)."),
      P("In designing journal bearings, the Sommerfeld number S is proportional to", "(ZN/P)(r/c)\u00B2", "Bearing characteristic", "Sommerfeld number S = (ZN/P)(r/c)\u00B2; at a given speed it indicates the operating regime (thick-film or thin-film with increasing friction)."),
      P("The ratio of bearing length to journal diameter for a new bearing design is normally taken as", "0.8 to 1.5", "Practical BO ratio", "Length-to-diameter ratio l/d is typically 0.8-1.5 for plain journal bearings - lower ratio reduces side oil leakage and keeps pressure moderate."),
      P("Welded joints designed against fatigue benefit from", "machined weld toe and grinding to remove stress raisers", "Improve Kf", "Grinding the weld toe, removing undercut/overlap and using butt rather than fillet welds reduce the stress concentration factor and raise fatigue life."),
      P("A fillet weld under tension/compression shear is designed for shear stress on the", "throat area (0.707 x s x l)", "Fillet throat dimension", "The throat thickness of a fillet weld = 0.707s where s is the weld size; the weld is loaded in shear on this throat area."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.production = [
      P("In sand casting the function of the riser (feeder) is to", "supply molten metal to compensate for shrinkage", "Feeding until solidification", "Risers stay hot and feed liquid metal into the solidifying casting to offset liquid and solidification shrinkage, preventing shrinkage cavities and porosity."),
      P("The gating ratio is defined as the ratio of", "sprue area: runner area: ingate area", "System of 1:2:4 or 1:4:2", "Gating ratio is A:As:Ar:Ag (sprue:runner:ingate). For non-pressurised systems the ingate is the largest (-1:4:2) to keep the metal calm."),
      P("A casting defect caused by mould gases trapped in the solidified skin is", "blowhole", "Gas entrapment", "Blowholes are smooth rounded holes from gas generated by sand moisture or reactions, trapped when the skin freezes before they escape."),
      P("Rapid chilling produces a 'chill' zone with", "fine dendrites", "Fast solidification", "Fast cooling at the mould wall produces a chilled surface of fine equiaxed/fine dendrite crystals; the interior cools more slowly and coarsens."),
      P("The overall shape of solidification front in grey iron gives rise to the", "'A' type (feathery) graphite pattern", "Oldoted solid front", "Grey iron's flat solidification front causes \u201CA\u201D type flake graphite distribution; other cooling patterns give \u201CB\u201D, \u201CD\u201D and \u201CE\u201D type graphite."),
      P("Pattern allowance added to dimensions for metal contraction is the", "shrinkage allowance", "Directional contraction", "Every metal shrinks while cooling from solidus to room temperature, so the pattern is made larger by the shrinkage (contraction) allowance plus machining, draft and distortion allowances."),
      P("A pattern carried along the mould surface following the parting plane is a", "split pattern", "Two-piece moulding", "For intricate shapes a split pattern is divided at the parting plane so each half can be drawn from its mould half; locating pins align the halves."),
      P("CO2 moulding employs", "sodium silicate binder hardened by CO2 gas", "Air-set binder chemistry", "In the CO2 process, sand is bonded with sodium silicate; pumping CO2 through the sand produces silica gel glue, giving a quick rigid mould able for ferrous and non-ferrous castings."),
      P("In shell moulding the sand is bonded with", "thermosetting resin and hardened at a hot pattern", "Phenolic resin", "A hot metal pattern is invested with a resin-sand mix which fuses into a thin shell; useful for accurate thin-section castings with smooth surfaces."),
      P("In investment (lost wax) casting, the pattern is", "consumed by melting out of the shell", "Wax pattern", "A wax pattern is coated with refractory slurry to form a shell, the wax is melted out, and metal poured into the cavity - giving exact detail like turbine blades."),
      P("In die casting the metal is forced into the die by", "high pressure (mechanical piston)", "Pressure casting", "Die casting injects molten metal into a steel die at high pressure, producing accurate, fine-surfaced parts in huge quantities; only for low-melting metals (zinc, aluminium, magnesium)."),
      P("Centrifugal casting is best suited for producing", "pipes, cylinders and rings", "Hollow axi-symmetric parts", "In true centrifugal casting the metal is spun about the axis, forcing slag and gas to the inner bore; suited for ring-shaped parts like water-main pipes and bearing bushes."),
      P("In arc welding the electrode is used as filler material when it is", "consumable (coated electrode/ wire)", "Filler through arc", "Consumable electrodes (SMAW, MIG/GMAW) melt to supply filler; non-consumable electrodes (TIG/GTAW) only strike the arc and a separate rod adds metal."),
      P("In gas welding the neutral flame is obtained with", "equal volumes of oxygen and acetylene", "Stoichiometric ratio", "With O2:C2H2 = 1:1 the inner cone is sharp and white and the flame is chemically neutral; excess acetylene gives a carburising flame, excess oxygen oxidising."),
      P("The shielding gas in MIG welding is usually", "argon or argon-CO2 mixtures", "Inert gas blanket", "MIG/GMAW uses argon (or argon + CO2/oxygen) to shield the molten pool from the atmosphere; a filler wire feeds continuously through the torch."),
      P("In resistance spot welding the plates are held and heated by", "electrodes applying pressure and current", "Lap resistance welding", "Two copper electrodes squeeze the lapped sheets and pass a large current; the resistance heat fuses a small spot between them."),
      P("Ultrasonic welding joins metals by", "high-frequency mechanical vibration with light pressure", "Sonotrode friction", "Holding parts under pressure and vibrating a sonotrode at ~20 kHz produces local frictional heat and cold welds without melting the bulk - ideal for foils and dissimilar metals."),
      P("The temperature at which a thermit mixture of iron oxide and aluminium ignites to weld rails is about", "3000\u00B0C", "Thermit reaction", "Aluminium reduces iron oxide exothermically; the molten steel formed at ~3000\u00B0C fills the joint gap between rails and fuses them together."),
      P("In oxy-fuel cutting the preheating flame ignites the metal which is then", "blown away by an oxygen jet", "Exothermic oxidation", "A small preheat flame raises the steel to its ignition temperature; a powerful oxygen jet oxidises it and mechanically blows the iron-oxide melt away, leaving a narrow kerf."),
      P("Flux used in submerged arc welding is spread as", "a granular blanket over the joint", "Melted below flux", "SAW feeds a bare wire under a heap of granulated flux; the arc burns beneath the flux, which melts to shield the pool. It gives deep penetration with high deposition."),
      P("Shot blasting is a cleaning process that removes scale and rust by", "propelling steel shots with compressed air or wheels", "Abrasive impact", "Shot blasting hurls steel shot/abrasive at the surface to remove scale, rust and coating residues before painting or further processing."),
      P("Pickling removes scale by", "acid immersion (H2SO4 or HCl)", "Chemical descaling", "Pickling dips steel in dilute sulphuric/hydrochloric acid which dissolves the oxide scale; rinsing and oiling follow to prevent re-rusting."),
      P("A single-point cutting tool with zero rake and zero clearance will", "rub instead of cutting", "Negative geometry", "Clearance/rake angles are essential; a zero-clearance tool cannot enter the work and a zero rake changes the force pattern - cutting calls for positive rake and clearance."),
      P("Tool wear by abrasion of hard particles is likely when machining", "with the presence of hard inclusions in the work material", "Abrasive wear", "Hard carbides, oxides and sand in the work material plough tiny grooves into the tool flank, the dominant erosive-abrasive wear mechanism in machining."),
      P("In a lathe, the feed direction of the tool for a plain turning operation is", "parallel to the axis of rotation", "Longitudinal feed", "Plain (straight) turning moves the tool parallel to the workpiece axis, reducing diameter along the length; facing moves it radially."),
      P("The size of a lathe is specified by the", "swing diameter over bed and distance between centres", "Bed capacity", "Lathe size is stated by the maximum diameter that can revolve over the bed (swing) and the maximum length between the chuck and tailstock centres."),
      P("In a capstan and turret lathe the turret holds", "six or more tools which index automatically", "Multi-tool turret", "The turret lathe's hexagonal turret carries drills, reamers, taps and forming tools on independent slides; indexing brings each tool into the work sequence rapidly."),
      P("The essential difference between capstan and turret lathe is that the capstan has", "a short stroke turret sliding on the saddle", "Saddle-mounted execution", "The capstan lathe's turret slides in a short stroke on the saddle for light work, whereas the turret lathe's turret is mounted directly on the saddle with a longer stroke for heavy work."),
      P("Climb (down) milling differs from conventional milling in that", "the cutter rotates in the same direction as the table feed", "Chip thickness starts thick", "In climb milling the peripheral cutter feeds along the motion of the work, chips start thick and thin to zero - better surface finish but needs backlash elimination."),
      P("In drilling, the tool rotates and the hole is produced by the", "point engagement of two cutting lips", "Twist-drill action", "A twist drill has two flutes with cutting lips at the point; the chisel edge at the centre does not cut but pushes the metal, causing high thrust at start."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.workshop = [
      P("The hardness of a chisel depends on the amount of", "carbon, hardened and tempered at the cutting zone", "Steel composition", "Chisels are made of high-carbon tool steel, hardened to a straw-blue temper at the point so the cutting edge remains hard yet the body tough."),
      P("A flat chisel is used for", "cutting flat surfaces and sheet metal", "General-purpose chiselling", "The flat chisel has a broad straight cutting edge for chipping, cutting sheet and removing excess material; cross-cut, diamond-point and round-nose chisels serve special shapes."),
      P("A half-round file has", "one flat and one curved face", "Cross-section shape", "The half-round file is flat on one side and half-round on the other - used for both flat work and internal curved (concave) surfaces."),
      P("In the filing process, the stroke direction gives a result that with the teeth biased to the right gives a push cut when the file is", "pushed forward with pressure", "File cuts on the push", "Files cut on the forward stroke; the teeth are raked forward and the file is lifted clear on the return to prevent dulling."),
      P("The safe edge of a file is", "an edge without teeth used to file close to a right-angle shoulder", "Guarding adjacent face", "A safe (uncut) edge lets the file work against a shoulder without scratching the adjacent finished surface - used for filing keyways and slots."),
      P("The most common angle of the hacksaw blade teeth is", "60\u00B0 (set at 45\u00B0-60\u00B0)", "Rake geometry", "Hacksaw teeth are set alternately or wavy to provide clearance; the standard rake is about 30\u00B0 leading with 60\u00B0 cutting/或 body angle."),
      P("The set of a hacksaw teeth refers to the", "alternate outward bending of teeth for clearance", "Kerb relief", "Setting bends alternate teeth slightly outward so the blade cuts a kerf wider than its body, preventing binding in the slot."),
      P("Scribing is marking lines with a", "needle or scriber, a hardened steel point", "Layout tool", "The scriber, a hardened steel point, draws the layout lines on the work surface; a surface plate and block help keep the lines straight."),
      P("A try square or engineer's square is used to test", "true perpendicular faces", "Right-angle accuracy", "An engineer's try square measures/verifies 90\u00B0 between adjacent faces and squares lines exactly at right angles during marking-out."),
      P("A vernier calliper reads to", "0.02 mm (with 50 divisions on the vernier)", "Least count formula", "Least count = 1 main scale division/number of vernier divisions = 0.5/25 = 0.02 mm; the metric vernier calliper reads to 0.02 mm."),
      P("A micrometer with a 25 to 50 mm range reads to", "0.01 mm on its main and thimble graduations", "Least count = pitch/divisions", "With a 0.5 mm pitch and 50 thimble divisions, the least count of a micrometer is 0.01 mm; the vernier micrometer refines this to 0.001 mm."),
      P("Tempering of a hardened tool is done to", "reduce hardness slightly and remove brittleness", "Brittleness relief", "Tempering reheats the hardened tool to below its transformation range and cools it, relieving internal stress and reducing brittleness while retaining cutting hardness."),
      P("Annealing of steel is the process of", "heating then cooling slowly in the furnace", "Softening treatment", "Annealing heats steel above its critical temperature, soaks, then cools very slowly (in the furnace), softening it for machining and removing internal stress."),
      P("Quenching a hardened steel in oil rather than water", "slows the cooling rate and reduces cracking", "Lower thermal shock", "Oil has a lower heat-transfer rate than water, giving a gentler quench that minimizes distortion, cracking and residual stress in the hardening of alloy steels."),
      P("In hardening steel the critical cooling rate must", "exceed the transformation rate to form martensite", "Avoid pearlite", "Hardening demands cooling faster than the critical rate so austenite transforms to martensite instead of pearlite/duallite; slower quench (air) does not harden."),
      P("In the rolling process, four-high rolling stands use small work rolls backed by", "large backup rolls", "Prevent bending", "Small work rolls reduce the contact area and roll force while large backup rolls prevent flexure, allowing thin strip to be rolled with accurate gauge."),
      P("Swaging is a metalworking process that", "reduces metal diameter/forms a taper by rotary hammering", "Rotary forging", "Swaging thins a rod or tube between rotating dies that hammer radially, producing tapers, recesses or pointed ends; it is a hot or cold rotary forging method."),
      P("Wire drawing must follow the precedent that", "each pass reduces the diameter so the drawn wire is smaller than the die entrance", "Plastic elongation", "In wire drawing the wire is pulled through successively smaller tapered dies, elongating and thinning it as the cross-section necks down."),
      P("In forging, the phenomenon of 'red-shortness' appears when", "sulphur is present in steel at forging temperature", "Dissolved sulphur", "Sulphur segregating in hot steel makes it hot-short (red-short) - brittle and likely to crack at forging heat. Manganese removes much of the harmful effect."),
      P("Upsetting in forging means", "increasing the cross-section by compressing a length of the stock axially", "Cross-section swell", "Upsetting hammers the end of a heated bar axially so the metal bulges and the cross-section increases - used for bolt heads and flanges."),
      P("The 'drawing out' operation in forging", "lengthens the stock and reduces the cross-section", "Draw-down", "Applying successive blows while moving the stock sideways (drawing out) lengthens the piece and thins the section, changing round stock into a smaller diameter."),
      P("The expansion of the air gap between mould halves in casting by a chilled insert is prevented by", "the chill itself producing a fine-grain layer", "Chill solidification", "Metal chills placed in the mould locally thicken the section and make the metal solidify quickly with a fine grain; the chill raises the local cooling rate rather than 'expanding gaps'."),
      P("The name 'fullering' is given to the forging die that", "shrinks or necks down part of a bar", "Neck-down tool", "The fullering die works metal into a groove, thinning and necking it before further drawing-out operations in hammer forging."),
      P("In the kitchen-ware and utensil pressing process, deep parts are formed by", "drawing (cup-making) rather than shallow pressing", "Deep drawing", "Deep drawing pulls a flat blank into a cup through a die with a punch, using blank-holder pressure; shallow stamping only flattens or forms shallow shapes."),
      P("The purpose of the 'blow-off' nozzle on a pneumatic chisel is to", "direct compressed air to clear chips while hammering", "Chip clearing jet", "The blow-off nozzle sends a controlled air jet at the cutting zone, clearing chips and cooling the tool so the chipping action stays productive."),
      P("Fettling of a casting removes", "runners, risers, flash and fins", "Post-casting dressing", "Fettling knocks off the gating system, risers, fins and flashes, then grinds gates to shape and completes surface cleaning of the casting."),
      P("The casting process that needs a furnace (crucible) melting only within the foundry and is ideal for art metalwork is", "investment (lost-wax) casting", "Small art castings", "Investment casting melts wax out of a ceramic shell for intricate, accurate castings - the process behind jewellery, art and turbine-blade work."),
      P("Forming by bending a sheet by pressing between a punch and die with the sheet resting on die edges is called", "'V' or 'air' bending", "Air/v-gap bending", "In air bending, the sheet spans die edges and the punch drives it into a V to the required angle; springback is allowed in tooling."),
      P("In shearing, the blades are set to give a small clearance because too much clearance causes", "a torn, rough edge with excessive burr", "Edges quality", "Blade clearance controls the fracture line; excessive clearance gives ragged, burred edges and higher, distorted fracture surfaces."),
      P("The tool used to seal the rivet shank end on the head side is a", "dolly bar (holding up)", "Supporting the head", "A dolly bar backs up the rivet head while the hammer or rivet set shapes the factory end, preventing damage to the formed head."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.industrial = [
      P("In PERT, the activity time estimate that represents the most likely duration is", "most likely time (m)", "Three-time estimate", "PERT uses optimistic (a), most likely (m) and pessimistic (b) times; expected time te = (a + 4m + b)/6, a weighted-average version moderate in risk."),
      P("In CPM, the critical path is the sequence of activities that has", "the longest total duration (minimum completion time)", "Determines project duration", "The critical path has zero float - any delay on it delays the whole project; it is found by joining the activities whose early and late times coincide."),
      P("The difference between the latest finish time and earliest finish time of an activity is its", "total float", "Scheduling slack", "Total float = LST - EST = LFT - EFT; it is the time an activity can be delayed without affecting overall project completion."),
      P("Free float of an activity is the time by which it may be delayed without affecting the", "start of a following activity", "Successor start", "Free float = EST of the following activity - EFT of the present activity; it does not delay succeeding activities (unlike total float)."),
      P("In the EOQ model with annual demand D, ordering cost O and holding cost Cc, the economic order quantity is", "Q = \u221A(2DO/Cc)", "Inventory square-root rule", "EOQ = \u221A(2DO/Cc) minimises the sum of ordering and holding costs; total annual cost = (D/Q)O + (Q/2)Cc."),
      P("ABC analysis of inventory classifies items into groups A B C by", "annual consumption value", "Pareto distribution", "Class A items have the highest annual usage value (~10% of items, 70% of value), B moderate and C numerous low-value items; A items get the tightest control."),
      P("The 'economic production quantity' model relaxes the EOQ assumption of", "instantaneous replenishment", "Finite production rate", "In the EPQ model production occurs at a finite rate p > demand d, giving Q = \u221A(2DO/[Cc(1-d/p)]), lower average stock than the instantaneous EOQ."),
      P("Just-In-Time (JIT) production aims to", "eliminate inventory by making only what is needed when needed", "Zero-stock pull", "JIT uses kanban cards to pull production, small lots and much reduced setup times so stock and WIP approach zero - eliminating waste rather than optimising lots."),
      P("The 5S methodology's second 'S' (Seiton) means", "set in order / sort out places", "Systematic arrangement", "The five S's: Seiri (sort), Seiton (set in order), Seiso (shine/clean), Seiketsu (standardise) and Shitsuke (sustain) - establishing a clean, organised workplace."),
      P("Poka-yoke in manufacturing means", "mistake-proofing to prevent defects at the source", "Error prevention", "Poka-yoke devices (pins, fixtures, sensors) make errors physically impossible or instantly detectable, used in Toyota's quality system to stop defects occurring."),
      P("Total productive maintenance (TPM) was pioneered by", "Nippon Denso (Toyota group) in the 1960-70s", "Reliability thinking", "TPM spread from Nippon Denso to all Toyota plants, involving operators in autonomous, planned maintenance so equipment uptime (OEE) stays high."),
      P("The Kaizen approach to improvement is best summarised as", "continuous, small, incremental improvements involving everyone", "Improvement culture", "Kaizen (good change) is the continuous improvement loop of standardise, do, check, act applied daily by all employees - not just one-off large projects."),
      P("In statistical quality control, the upper and lower control limits of an X-bar chart are set at", "\u03BC \u00B1 3\u03C3/\u221An", "Process limits", "Control limits = \u03C3/ + 3\u03C3x where \u03C3x = \u03C3/\u221An. Points outside these limits signal an assignable cause requiring investigation."),
      P("A control chart for the fraction defective in a lot is the", "p-chart", "Attribute proportion chart", "The p-chart monitors the proportion of defectives per sample (attribute data); the c-chart counts defects per unit. Both have LCL/UCL from binomial/Poisson statistics."),
      P("The normal distribution curve for control-chart purposes has the property that", "99.73% of the values lie within \u00B13\u03C3", "Empirical rule", "Within \u00B1\u03C3 about 68%, \u00B12\u03C3 about 95% and \u00B13\u03C3 about 99.73% of the observations lie - the basis of 3-sigma limits."),
      P("Acceptance sampling as an inspection technique", "judges whole lots from sample results against the AQL", "Lot-by-lot judgment", "Acceptance sampling decides whether to accept or reject a whole lot on the strength of the number of defectives found in a random sample, specified by the OC curve and AQL."),
      P("In sampling inspection, the operating characteristic (OC) curve plots", "probability of accepting a lot against the lot fraction defective", "Producer's/consumer's risk", "The OC curve shows Pa for each lot fraction defective p; AQL defines the producer's risk point and LTPD the consumer's risk point."),
      P("Line balancing aims to", "make station times equal so the line throughput is maximum at the cycle time", "Balanced flow", "Line balancing redistributes work elements among stations so the largest station time (cycle time C) is a minimum, minimising idle time and maximise output."),
      P("The precedence relationship in line balancing is portrayed by a", "precedence diagram", "Job dependency graph", "A precedence diagram shows which work elements must precede others; balancing assigns elements to stations respecting those predecessor constraints."),
      P("Materials requirement planning (MRP) is driven by the", "master production schedule through the bill of materials", "Requirements explosion", "MRP takes the MPS, explodes the BOM by lead times, and nets against on-hand inventory to time-order components and assemblies for manufacturing."),
      P("In forecasting, the simple moving average with long period responds to demand changes", "slowly and smoothly", "Lag effect", "Longer moving-average periods smooth out noise but lag behind shifts in demand; shorter periods react faster with more variability."),
      P("Exponential smoothing places", "more weight on recent data with a smoothing constant \u03B1 (0< \u03B1<1)", "Recency weighting", "Forecast Ft = \u03B1At-1 + (1-\u03B1)Ft-1; a high \u03B1 reacts quickly to recent changes while a low \u03B1 gives a flatter, more stable forecast."),
      P("Inventory carrying cost consists mainly of", "capital tied up, storage, insurance and obsolescence", "Holding expense", "Carrying (holding) cost includes interest on investment, storage space, insurance, taxes, deterioration and obsolescence - typically 20-30% of item value per year."),
      P("The spiral of reducing setup time to economical batches is achieved by the", "single-minute exchange of die (SMED)", "Setup shrink", "SMED reduces die-change times to under ten minutes by separating internal and external setup, using quick clamps and pre-parked tooling - enabling small-lot JIT production."),
      P("The 'bowl' or 'convex' distribution in safety-stock allocation overexpresses that", "more safety stock is placed at the centre of a serial line", "Buffer placement", "In a serial production line, safety stock is most effective near the bottleneck; optimal buffer placement follows a convex ('bowl') pattern along the line."),
      P("Work study breaks into method study and", "time (work) measurement", "Two branches", "Work study = method study (improve the way) + work measurement (measure the time). Both use stopwatch study, PMTS and activity sampling."),
      P("Standard time in time study is", "normal time plus allowances", "Rating and rest", "Standard time = normal time (observed x rating) + allowances for rest, personal needs, interference and unavoidable delays, expressed as a percentage of normal time."),
      P("The main objective of maintenance management is", "to maximise equipment availability at minimum total cost", "Availability-cost balance", "Maintenance balances preventive/periodic effort against breakdowns to maximise plant availability and reduce downtime and repair cost combined."),
      P("Breakdown maintenance means work carried out", "only after a failure occurs", "Reactive repair", "Breakdown (reactive) maintenance repairs equipment only when it fails - cheapest to run but risks long downtime and production loss; preventive maintenance schedules replacement before failure."),
      P("In the 'make or buy' decision the decisive comparison is", "the marginal (incremental) cost of making versus the purchase price", "Incremental analysis", "The firm compares the incremental variable costs of internal production with the supplier's price, including capacity, technology and quality considerations."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.maths = [
      P("If A is a 3x3 matrix with eigenvalues 1, 2, and 3, the determinant of A is", "6", "Product of eigenvalues", "Determinant = product of eigenvalues = 1 x 2 x 3 = 6. The trace is their sum = 6."),
      P("If a matrix has a zero eigenvalue, then the matrix is", "singular", "Non-invertible condition", "A zero eigenvalue means det(A) = 0, so A is singular and its rows/columns are linearly dependent (does not have an inverse)."),
      P("The derivative of ln(x\u00B2 + 1) is", "2x/(x\u00B2 + 1)", "Chain rule", "d/dx[ln f(x)] = f'(x)/f(x) = 2x/(x\u00B2+1). Uses the logarithm chain rule."),
      P("The value of the integral \u222B\u2080\u00B9 x\u00B2 dx is", "1/3", "Power rule", "\u222B x\u00B2 dx = x\u00B3/3 evaluated from 0 to 1 = 1/3."),
      P("The Laplace transform of the unit step function u(t) is", "1/s", "Standard transform", "L{u(t)} = \u222B\u2080^inf e^(-st) dt = 1/s for Re(s) > 0. L{t^n} = n!/s^(n+1)."),
      P("The Laplace transform of e^(-at) is", "1/(s + a)", "Exponential transform", "L{e^(-at)} = \u222B e^(-(s+a)t) dt = 1/(s+a), valid for Re(s) > -a."),
      P("The inverse Laplace transform of 1/(s\u00B2 + \u03C9\u00B2) is", "(1/\u03C9) sin(\u03C9t)", "Sine transform pair", "L{sin(\u03C9t)} = \u03C9/(s\u00B2+\u03C9\u00B2), so L^{-1}{1/(s\u00B2+\u03C9\u00B2)} = (1/\u03C9) sin(\u03C9t)."),
      P("The probability of getting exactly 3 heads in 5 tosses of a fair coin is", "10/32 = 5/16", "Binomial distribution", "P = C(5,3)(1/2)\u2075 = 10/32 = 5/16. The binomial coefficient C(n,k) = n!/(k!(n-k)!)."),
      P("For a Poisson distribution the mean is equal to", "the variance (both = \u03BB)", "Equal moments", "For Poisson, the mean and variance are both \u03BB. For the binomial, mean = np and variance = npq."),
      P("The correlation coefficient of two independent variables is", "0", "Zero covariation", "Independence implies covariance zero, hence the correlation coefficient r = cov(X,Y)/(\u03C3x\u03C3y) = 0 (the converse is not necessarily true)."),
      P("The coefficient of variation is the", "ratio of standard deviation to the mean", "Relative dispersion", "CV = (\u03C3/\u03BC) x 100% - a normalised (unit-free) measure of relative dispersion, useful to compare series with different means."),
      P("Simpson's rule with three ordinates (parabolic strips) integrates f(x) over a and b with spacing h as", "(h/3)[y0 + 4y1 + y2]", "1/3 rule", "Simpson's 1/3 rule: \u222B y dx \u2248 (h/3)[y0 + 4y1 + 2y2 + ... + 4y(n-1) + yn] for even n; it is exact for cubic polynomials."),
      P("The trapezoidal rule integrates with", "straight-line segments between ordinates", "First-order approximation", "Trapezoidal rule \u222B y dx = h/2 [y0 + 2y1 + 2y2 + ... + yn]; more accurate than the rectangle rule but less accurate than Simpson's for smooth functions."),
      P("The second derivative at the midpoint can be approximated by the central difference", "(f(x+h) - 2f(x) + f(x-h))/h\u00B2", "Finite difference", "Central difference f''(x) \u2248 [f(x+h) - 2f(x) + f(x-h)]/h\u00B2 with error O(h\u00B2); forward and backward forms have O(h) accuracy."),
      P("The Fourier series converges to the midpoint of a jump discontinuity", "the average of the left and right limits", "Dirichlet condition", "At a discontinuity the Fourier series converges to (f(x-)+f(x+))/2, the midpoint of the jump, as guaranteed by the Dirichlet conditions."),
      P("The Fourier transform of a rectangular pulse becomes an", "sinc function", "Transform pair", "The rectangular pulse x(t) = 1 for |t|<T transforms to 2T sinc(fT) = 2T sin(\u03C0fT)/(\u03C0fT) - the sinc centred on the origin."),
      P("The gradient of the scalar field \u03C6 = x\u00B2 + y\u00B2 is", "2x i + 2y j", "Vector derivative", "grad \u03C6 = (\u2202\u03C6/\u2202x)i + (\u2202\u03C6/\u2202y)j = 2xi + 2yj. The divergence treats dot products of vectors."),
      P("Divergence of the radial vector field F = x i + y j + z k is", "3", "div F = \u2207\u00B7F", "div F = \u2202x/\u2202x + \u2202y/\u2202y + \u2202z/\u2202z = 3. The curl of a conservative (gradient) field is zero."),
      P("An integrating factor that makes the differential equation (x\u00B2+y\u00B2)dx + 2xy dy = 0 exact is", "1/x\u00B2", "Exactness by denominator", "Dividing by x\u00B2 gives (1 + y\u00B2/x\u00B2)dx + (2y/x)dy = 0 which satisfies \u2202M/\u2202y = \u2202N/\u2202x = 2y/x\u00B2, so the equation becomes exact."),
      P("The rank of the matrix [[1,2],[4,8]] is", "1", "Dependent rows", "The second row is 4 times the first, so the two rows are linearly dependent and the rank is 1."),
      P("For the linear system x + y = 3 and 2x + 2y = 6, the solution set is", "infinite (dependent equations)", "Consistent dependent", "Both equations represent the same line, so there are infinitely many solutions; a system with a zero determinant but consistent RHS is dependent."),
      P("The characteristic equation of the 2x2 matrix [[1,2],[3,4]] is", "\u03BB\u00B2 - 5\u03BB - 2 = 0", "Trace and determinant", "The characteristic equation is \u03BB\u00B2 - (trace)\u03BB + det = 0 = \u03BB\u00B2 - 5\u03BB - 2 = 0; eigenvalues are the roots."),
      P("The limit of (sin x)/x as x \u2192 0 is", "1", "Standard limit", "By l'Hopital or series expansion, lim (sin x)/x = 1 as x\u21920; sin x = x - x\u00B3/3! + ... ."),
      P("The shortest distance from the plane 2x - 2y + z = 12 to the origin is", "4 units", "Plane distance formula", "d = |Ax\u2080+By\u2080+Cz\u2080+D|/\u221A(A\u00B2+B\u00B2+C\u00B2) = 12/\u221A(4+4+1) = 12/3 = 4."),
      P("The equation of the plane parallel to 2x+3y-z=5 through the point (1,1,1) is", "2x+3y-z = 4", "Constant shift", "A parallel plane has the same normal: 2x+3y-z = 2+3-1 = 4 passing through (1,1,1)."),
      P("For a linearly elastic spring, the work done in stretching it by x against a force kx is", "kx\u00B2/2", "Area under force curve", "W = \u222B\u2080^x kx dx = kx\u00B2/2, the triangular area under the force-displacement line."),
      P("If y = e^(2t) satisfies y'' + by' + c y = 0, then y' and y'' are", "2e^(2t) and 4e^(2t)", "Exponential solution", "y' = 2e^(2t), y'' = 4e^(2t); substituting requires 4 + 2b + c = 0 for a solution."),
      P("The volume enclosed by the sphere x\u00B2+y\u00B2+z\u00B2 = R\u00B2 is", "4\u03C0R\u00B3/3", "Solid geometry", "The sphere of radius R has volume 4\u03C0R\u00B3/3; its surface area is 4\u03C0R\u00B2."),
      P("The mean of a binomial distribution with n trials and probability of success p is", "np", "Binomial mean", "Binomial mean = np, variance = npq where q = 1-p. Poisson mean = variance = \u03BB."),
      P("The maximum value of f(x) = 4x - x\u00B2 occurs at x =", "2", "Stationary point", "f'(x) = 4 - 2x = 0 gives x = 2, and f''(x) = -2 < 0 confirms a maximum; f(2) = 4."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.emech = [
      P("The resultant of two equal forces P meeting at an angle \u03B1 is", "2P cos(\u03B1/2)", "Force parallelogram", "R = \u221A(P\u00B2+P\u00B2+2P\u00B2cos\u03B1) = P\u221A(2(1+cos\u03B1)) = 2P cos(\u03B1/2). At \u03B1 = 0 the resultant is 2P; at 180\u00B0 it is zero."),
      P("The moment of a force about a point is the product of the", "force and the perpendicular distance from the point", "M = F.d", "Moment of force = F x d where d is the perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action; its unit is N m."),
      P("A couple consists of two", "equal, parallel and opposite forces separated by a distance", "Two equal parallel opposite forces", "A couple has zero resultant force but a constant moment F x d; its moment is the same about any point."),
      P("The resultant of a force system is the", "single force and couple that equivalently replace them", "Equivalent system", "Any system of forces reduces to a resultant force acting through an arbitrary point plus a resultant couple; both together are the complete equivalent."),
      P("Lami's theorem applies to a body under", "three coplanar concurrent forces in equilibrium", "Three-force equilibrium", "For three coplanar concurrent forces, each force is proportional to the sine of the angle between the other two: P/sin\u03B1 = Q/sin\u03B2 = R/sin\u03B3."),
      P("Coplanar concurrent forces at equilibrium require", "the algebraic sum of components in x and y to be zero", "Closed force polygon", "Equilibrium \u03A3Fx = 0 and \u03A3Fy = 0 for concurrent coplanar forces; the force polygon closes."),
      P("Moment of inertia of a rectangle of base b and height d about its base centroidal axis is", "bd\u00B3/12", "Standard area moment", "Ixx about the centroidal axis = bd\u00B3/12; about the base (edge) it is bd\u00B3/3 by the parallel axis theorem."),
      P("Moment of inertia of a solid circular section of diameter d about an axis through its centre is", "\u03C0d\u2074/64", "Circle inertia", "Ix = Iy = \u03C0d\u2074/64 for a solid circle; the polar moment is \u03C0d\u2074/32 = 2I."),
      P("The radius of gyration of a body of area A and moment of inertia I is", "\u221A(I/A)", "k = \u221A(I/A)", "Radius of gyration k = \u221A(I/A). For a rod pivoted at one end, the compound-pendulum length is k\u00B2/x + x."),
      P("A body sliding on a rough horizontal plane with coefficient of friction \u03BC is retarded by", "\u03BCg", "Friction retardation", "Friction force = \u03BCmg, so deceleration a = \u03BCmg/m = \u03BCg, independent of mass."),
      P("The angle of repose of a granular material is the angle at which", "the material just begins to slide under its own weight", "Friction balance", "tan(angle of repose) = \u03BC, so grains or a body rest on a slope less than this angle and slide when exceeded since the driving component mg sin\u03B8 exceeds friction."),
      P("For equilibrium on a rough inclined plane of angle \u03B1, a body of weight W is just on the point of moving up when the applied horizontal force P is", "P = W tan(\u03B1 + \u03C6)", "Impending upward motion", "Resolving gives P = W tan(\u03B1+\u03C6) to drag the body up, and P = W tan(\u03C6 - \u03B1) to prevent sliding down, where \u03C6 is the friction angle."),
      P("The law of machine for a lifting system relates", "the effort and the load pulled", "Effort-load relation", "The law of a machine is P = mW + C relating effort P to load W; mechanical advantage MA = W/P and efficiency = MA/VR."),
      P("The screw jack's velocity ratio with lead l and effort arm R is", "2\u03C0R/l", "Effort travel over lead", "VR = distance moved by effort/distance moved by load = 2\u03C0R/l; efficiency considers friction in the screw threads."),
      P("In a gear train, the velocity ratio is the ratio of", "the angular velocity of the driver to that of the follower", "Input/output speeds", "Velocity ratio = \u03C9driver/\u03C9follower. In a compound train it equals the product of driving-teeth divided by driven-teeth terms in each mesh."),
      P("In a compound gear train, the driver rotates at 240 rpm and drives a 120-tooth gear through a 40-tooth intermediate. The output speed is", "80 rpm", "N1z1 = N2z2", "N2 = N1 x z1/z2 = 240 x 40/120 = 80 rpm at the intermediate; the direction reverses each mesh."),
      P("The acceleration due to gravity at the equator is slightly lower than at the poles because", "the Earth bulges and rotates (centrifugal effect)", "Rotation and flattening", "Both the greater equatorial radius (weaker gravity) and the centrifugal acceleration from rotation reduce g at the equator; g \u2248 9.78 m/s\u00B2 there vs 9.83 at the poles."),
      P("Projectile motion follows a path that is", "parabolic (ignoring air resistance)", "Constant horizontal velocity", "With vx constant and vy uniformly accelerated by gravity, the trajectory is a parabola; maximum height H = v\u00B2 sin\u00B2\u03B8/(2g)."),
      P("Time of flight of a projectile thrown at 30\u00B0 with velocity 40 m/s is", "4.08 s approximately", "T = 2v sin\u03B8/g", "T = 2v sin\u03B8/g = 2 x 40 x 0.5/9.81 = 4.08 s. The horizontal range R = v\u00B2 sin2\u03B8/g."),
      P("A metre rule pivoted at the 50 cm mark in equilibrium horizontally has its centre of gravity at the", "50 cm mark", "Balanced rule", "The metre rule balances at its geometric centre when uniform, so the c.g. lies at the 50 cm (midpoint) position for equilibrium."),
      P("If three forces keep a particle in equilibrium, they must be", "concurrent (and coplanar)", "Concurrency condition", "Three forces in equilibrium must be concurrent (their lines of action meet) and coplanar unless their sum is zero identically; the polygon closes."),
      P("The principle of moments states that for equilibrium", "the sum of clockwise moments equals the sum of anticlockwise moments", "Moment balance", "Equilibrium \u03A3M = 0 about any point means total clockwise moment = total anticlockwise moment, the basis of beam reaction analysis."),
      P("Simply supported beam with a uniformly distributed load of intensity w over a span L: the maximum bending moment is", "wL\u00B2/8", "Standard UDL moment", "BM max = wL\u00B2/8 at mid-span for a simply supported uniform load; reactions are wL/2 each."),
      P("Cantilever with a uniformly distributed load of intensity w over its full length L: the maximum moment is", "wL\u00B2/2 at the fixed end", "UDL cantilever", "For a cantilever with UDL, M = wL\u00B2/2 at the fixed support, reducing linearly to zero at the free end."),
      P("The reaction at each support of a simply supported beam of span L carrying a central point load W is", "W/2", "Symmetric reactions", "By symmetry each end reaction is W/2; the bending moment at the load is WL/4."),
      P("Kinetic energy of a body of mass m moving at velocity v is", "mv\u00B2/2", "Energy of motion", "KE = mv\u00B2/2. Work done by a force equals the change in kinetic energy of the body it acts on."),
      P("The work done by a constant force F acting through a distance s in its own direction is", "Fs", "Work definition", "Work W = F.s (force along displacement). Work against friction, gravity or a spring transfers energy accordingly."),
      P("Impulse is defined as", "force multiplied by the time for which it acts", "F.t", "Impulse = F x t = change in momentum m\u0394v; both are measured in N s (kg m/s). For a rocket or hammer, the impulse governs the velocity change."),
      P("In elastic collisions between equal masses, the bodies", "exchange velocities", "Momentum and energy", "For an elastic collision of two equal masses with one initially at rest, the moving mass stops and transfers its full velocity to the other - velocities exchange."),
      P("The loss of kinetic energy in a perfectly inelastic collision between two equal masses m moving at v each toward each other is", "mv\u00B2", "Energy lost to deformation", "Total KE before = 2 x (1/2 mv\u00B2) = mv\u00B2; after sticking they stop (momentum zero), so all mv\u00B2 is lost in heat and deformation."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.materials = [
      P("The most commonly occurring element in plain carbon steel by weight is iron, with carbon between", "0.04% and 2.14%", "Steel definition", "Steel contains 0.04-2.14% carbon; below 0.04% is wrought iron, above 2.14% (up to ~4.3%) is cast iron."),
      P("The eutectoid composition of plain carbon steel is", "0.8% carbon", "Pearlite point", "The eutectoid of the Fe-Fe\u2083C system is 0.8%C; eutectoid (pearlite) forms at 727\u00B0C. Steels below 0.8% are hypoeutectoid, above hyper-eutectoid."),
      P("Martensite is a", "body-centred tetragonal (BCT) supersaturated solid solution of carbon in iron", "Hard phase", "Rapid quenching traps carbon, distorting BCC ferrite into BCT martensite - very hard and brittle, usually tempered for ductility."),
      P("Pearlite is a lamellar mixture of", "ferrite and cementite", "Eutectoid aggregate", "Pearlite is a lamellar (layered) aggregate of ferrite (a) and cementite (Fe\u2083C) of eutectoid composition formed at 727\u00B0C on slow cooling."),
      P("Austenite is", "gamma iron with dissolved carbon (FCC)", "High-temperature phase", "Austenite is FCC gamma iron that dissolves up to about 2.14% carbon; it is non-magnetic and forms the starting phase for hardening treatments."),
      P("Spheroidising annealing is applied to", "high-carbon tool steels to improve machinability", "Carbide spheroidisation", "Prolonged holding just below A1 spheroïdises the cementite lamellae into globular particles, softening the steel so cutting tools can be machined."),
      P("Full annealing consists of", "heating above A3 (or A1), then cooling slowly in the furnace", "Softening heat treatment", "Full annealing above the upper critical line, followed by furnace cooling, gives coarse pearlite of maximum softness and ductility."),
      P("Normalising of steel is done by", "heating above A3 and cooling in still air", "Air cooling", "Normalising air-cools from above A3, producing fine pearlite with better strength and hardness than annealed steel while relieving stress."),
      P("Tempering of quenched martensite reduces", "brittleness and internal stresses while retaining hardness", "Brittleness control", "Tempering at 150-650\u00B0C transforms brittle martensite into tempered martensite (troostite/sorbite) with controlled hardness-toughness balance."),
      P("Hardenability of steel is measured by the", "Jominy end-quench test", "Hardenability curve", "In the Jominy test a bar is quenched from one end and hardness plotted against distance from the quenched end, giving the hardenability curve (depth of hardening)."),
      P("Austempering produces", "bainite - a tough, stress-free microstructure", "Isothermal transformation", "Austempering quenches to a temperature above Ms and holds so austenite transforms isothermally to bainite, giving high toughness with little distortion."),
      P("Martempering (marquenching)", "quenches to just above Ms, then air-cools so martensite forms uniformly", "Two-step quench", "Marquenching arrests cooling above Ms, equalising temperature before final transformation, reducing distortion and cracking."),
      P("The microstructure at the eutectoid point during isothermal transformation at 550\u00B0C (about halfway) is", "upper bainite (feathery)", "TTT diagram", "At 550\u00B0C the nose of the TTT diagram produces fine pearlite; upper bainite grows at slightly lower temperatures as feathery ferrite with carbide."),
      P("Ferritic malleable cast iron is produced by", "graphitising (annealing) white cast iron", "White to ductile", "Malleable iron starts as white iron which anneals so carbon precipitates as nodular temper graphite - giving ductility for the cast product."),
      P("Nodular (SG) cast iron obtains its graphite in", "spheroidal form by magnesium/ cerium treatment", "Spheroidisation", "Adding Mg or Ce to low-sulphur iron makes the graphite grow as spheroids, giving SG (ductile) iron with good strength and ductility."),
      P("The addition of silicon to cast iron", "promotes graphitisation and weakens/free carbon above the alloy", "Graphite promoter", "Silicon encourages carbon to separate as graphite during solidification rather than cementite - the basis of grey and ductile irons."),
      P("The primary function of chromium in stainless steel is to", "form a protective oxide film (passivation) resisting corrosion", "Corrosion resistance", "About 12%+ chromium forms a self-healing Cr\u2082O\u2083 film; martensitic, ferritic and austenitic grades all rely on it."),
      P("Austenitic stainless steel such as 18/8 contains", "18% chromium and 8% nickel", "Type 304", "The 18/8 austenitic stainless (type 304) has ~18% Cr and ~8% Ni, giving FCC austenite at room temperature and superior formability/corrosion resistance."),
      P("The most important property of molybdenum as an alloying element", "raises the hardenability and high-temperature strength", "Heat resistance", "Molybdenum elevates hardenability (allows oil quenching), raises resistance to tempering and retains creep strength at elevated temperatures in tool and creep-resistant steels."),
      P("Tool steels generally contain tungsten, vanadium or cobalt to", "retain hardness and resist wear at high cutting temperatures", "Hot hardness", "Alloy elements (W, V, Co) hold the tool's hardness at red heat (hot hardness) while molybdenum and chromium improve hardenability."),
      P("The process of carburising a low-carbon steel", "adds carbon to the surface by diffusion", "Case hardening", "Carburising exposes the steel to a carbon-rich atmosphere so the surface case gains 0.8%+ carbon which is then hardened; the core stays tough."),
      P("Nitriding differs from carburising because it", "uses nitrogen without heating near A1 and needs no quench", "Nitride hardening", "Nitriding in an ammonia atmosphere at ~500\u00B0C forms hard nitrides with alloy elements (Al, Cr, Mo) - harder than carburised cases with lower distortion."),
      P("The property of a metal that resists indentation is", "hardness", "Indentation resistance", "Hardness tests (Brinell, Rockwell, Vickers) measure resistance to local plastic indentation; hardness correlates with wear resistance and strength."),
      P("Ductility is the property of a material that allows", "large plastic deformation before fracture", "Deformation before failure", "Ductility is the ability to deform plastically (measured by % elongation and reduction of area in tension); its opposite is brittleness."),
      P("Young's modulus of steel is about", "200 GPa", "Elastic constant", "Steel tensile modulus E \u2248 200 GPa (200,000 N/mm\u00B2); aluminium ~70 GPa, copper ~110 GPa, cast iron ~100-170 GPa."),
      P("A superheated steam pipe line withstands creep best in", "molybdenum-chromium low-alloy steel", "Creep resistance", "Mo-Cr steels retain creep strength up to ~540\u00B0C, resisting slow plastic flow (creep) in high-temperature steam plant."),
      P("Bronze is fundamentally an alloy of", "copper and tin", "Bronze composition", "Bronze = copper + tin. Brass = copper + zinc. Both may contain small quantities of lead, nickel and phosphorus for special properties."),
      P("The aluminium alloy with copper as the principal addition where ageing precipitation hardens it is", "duralumin (2xxx series)", "Age-hardening alloy", "Duralumin (~4% Cu, ~0.5% Mg, ~0.5% Mn) is solution-treated, quenched and aged to precipitate CuAl\u2082 - gaining strength for aircraft skin."),
      P("White metal (babbitt) bearings are", "tin- or lead-based alloys with antimony and copper", "Bearing alloy", "Babbitt is a soft white ternary alloy (Sn/Sb/Cu or Pb/Sb/Sn) with low melting point, used as a thin lining to embed dirt in sliding bearings."),
      P("The failure of a metal under repeated alternating stress is called", "fatigue failure", "Cyclic stress failure", "Fatigue fractures build from stress raisers under repeated loads far below the static strength; the endurance limit is the stress the metal survives forever."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.rac = [
      P("The standard unit of refrigeration is a", "ton of refrigeration = 3.517 kW", "Cooling rate", "One ton of refrigeration is the rate required to melt 1 ton of ice in 24 hours = 3320 kcal/24h = 3.517 kW; it is 12000 BTU/h."),
      P("In the vapour-compression cycle, the refrigerant enters the evaporator as a", "low-pressure wet (two-phase) mixture", "Expansion-valve discharge", "After the expansion valve the refrigerant is a low-pressure two-phase mixture (mostly liquid with some flash vapour); it boils in the evaporator absorbing heat."),
      P("The coefficient of performance (COP) of a domestic refrigerator in terms of heat removed per work input is", "QL/W", "Cooling duty ratio", "COP_ref = heat removed from the cold space QL divided by the compressor work W. The heat pump COP = QH/W = COP_ref + 1."),
      P("In a simple vapour-compression system the compressor discharge is", "high-pressure superheated vapour", "Compressor outlet state", "The compressor raises pressure and temperature, discharging superheated vapour to the condenser; after the condenser it is a subcooled liquid."),
      P("The main advantage of using a multistage (compound) vapour-compression system is to", "reduce power and raise COP for large temperature lifts", "Two-stage compression", "Compound compression with intercooling restricts the temperature rise per stage, reducing volumetric efficiency loss and compressor power at low evaporator temperatures."),
      P("A flash chamber between two expansion stages", "separates flash vapour from liquid at an intermediate pressure", "Flash gas removal", "The flash intercooler lets saturated-vapour flash off and mix into the second-stage suction, while the liquid continues to the low-stage evaporator."),
      P("The cascade refrigeration cycle uses", "two separate refrigerant circuits coupled by a cascade condenser", "Low and high-temperature circuits", "In a cascade plant, a high-temperature circuit cools the condenser of a low-temperature circuit, each using its own refrigerant suited to the temperature level."),
      P("For a given evaporator and condenser, the compressor work increases when the", "condenser temperature rises or evaporator temperature falls", "Larger lift", "Both raising TH and lowering TL widen the temperature/pressure lift, increasing specific work and lowering the COP of the reversed cycle."),
      P("In absorption refrigeration, the function of the generator is to", "desorb refrigerant vapour from the rich solution by heating", "Boil off refrigerant", "The generator heats the rich ammonia/water (or LiBr/water) solution, driving off refrigerant vapour; the absorber dissolves refrigerant back to maintain the cascade."),
      P("In a LiBr-water absorption system, the refrigerant is", "water", "Water boils at vacuum pressure", "In the LiBr-water system, water acts as refrigerant and lithium bromide solution as absorbent; it is widely used in large chillers driven by waste heat."),
      P("In an ammonia-water absorption system, ammonia is the", "refrigerant and water the absorbent", "Natural refrigeration pair", "Ammonia is the refrigerant (high latent heat, no ozone damage) and water is the absorbent; used in industrial and rural gas-fired refrigerators."),
      P("Evaporative cooling ('air washer') works well when the", "wet-bulb depression (relative humidity) is large", "Desert cooling", "Evaporative coolers humidify air and drop its dry-bulb temperature toward wet-bulb; their effectiveness is greatest in hot dry climates where the wet-bulb temperature is low."),
      P("The cooling load of an air-conditioned room includes sensible and latent heat from", "occupants, appliances, lights, infiltration and solar gain", "Heat sources", "The design cooling load sums sensible heat (walls, glass, roof, equipment, occupants' convection) plus latent heat (moisture from people, infiltration, food) to select the plant capacity."),
      P("By-pass factor (BPF) of a cooling coil is the", "fraction of air that passes over the coil without being conditioned", "Bypass fraction", "BPF describes the air that completely bypasses contact with the coil; contact factor (CF) = 1 - BPF. Lower BPF means more effective coil."),
      P("An air-conditioning plant achieves a dew-point temperature corresponding to the", "apparatus dew point (ADP) of the cooling coil surface", "Coil surface temperature", "The coil's surface temperature is the apparatus dew point; the air is cooled toward ADP and moisture condenses, giving supply air near saturated at ADP."),
      P("In a psychrometric chart the horizontal axis represents", "dry-bulb temperature", "Dry-bulb scale", "Psychrometric axes: x = dry-bulb temperature, near-vertical curves = enthalpy, sloping = specific humidity (moisture content), curved lines = relative humidity and the wet-bulb follows diagonal lines."),
      P("Relative humidity is the ratio of", "the actual partial pressure of water vapour to the saturation pressure at the same temperature", "Vapour pressure ratio", "RH = pv/ps x 100%. Saturated air has 100% RH. Humidity ratio is the mass of water per kilogram of dry air."),
      P("Specific humidity (humidity ratio) of moist air is", "kg of water vapour per kg of dry air", "Mass ratio", "Humidity ratio w = 0.622 pv/(p - pv) kg/kg of dry air. It does not change with temperature alone - only moisture addition/removal changes it."),
      P("On a psychrometric chart, sensible cooling of air is represented by a line that runs", "horizontally leftward (constant humidity ratio)", "Sensible-only", "Sensible cooling moves left at constant humidity ratio; latent cooling (dehumidifying) moves downward at constant dry-bulb; cooling with dehumidification moves down-left."),
      P("A cooling tower performs its duty primarily by", "evaporating a part of the water to cool the rest", "Evaporative cooling", "In a cooling tower, a small fraction of the circulating water evaporates, absorbing heat and cooling the main body down to near the wet-bulb temperature of ambient air."),
      P("The optimum number of compressor stages for a given temperature lift is the one that", "minimises total compression work", "Equal pressure ratio per stage", "For an ideal gas the pressure ratio per stage is chosen equal ((ratio)^(1/n)) so the work split is even and total work is minimised, improving COP against mechanical and volumetric losses."),
      P("Superheating the refrigerant suction", "protects the compressor from liquid slugging", "Suction safety", "A little superheat ensures the compressor takes dry vapour, preventing damage from liquid entering the cylinder; too much superheat reduces the evaporator's useful cooling."),
      P("Subcooling the liquid refrigerant before the expansion valve", "increases the refrigerating effect", "Liquid subcooling", "Subcooling lowers the enthalpy before throttling, increasing the enthalpy drop across the evaporator and raising refrigeration capacity for the same power."),
      P("In a hermetic compressor the motor is", "sealed inside the compressor casing with the refrigerant", "Fully sealed unit", "Hermetic compressors enclose motor and pump in one welded shell so there is no shaft seal leakage; used widely in domestic refrigerators and small AC units."),
      P("In a rotary vane compressor, the vapour is compressed by", "rotating vanes sweeping a displaced volume", "Rotary displacement", "Rotor vanes split the crescent-shaped chamber; as they rotate they sweep gas from suction to discharge, compressing it. Quiet, compact, oil-sealed, used in car AC and domestic systems."),
      P("The purpose of the accumulator in a refrigeration plant is to", "separate liquid carry-over from the suction line", "Protect the compressor", "The accumulator at the evaporator outlet traps liquid slugs and feeds only vapour to the compressor suction, preventing compressor damage at low loads."),
      P("Defrosting of an evaporator operating below 0\u00B0C is required because", "frost layer acts as an insulation reducing the heat-transfer rate", "Frost resistance", "A growing ice/frost layer increases the thermal resistance and airflow blockage, so electric, hot-gas or water defrost cycles are scheduled."),
      P("Dew point temperature is the temperature at which", "moisture just begins to condense at the existing humidity ratio", "Condensation onset", "Cooled unsaturated air reaches saturation at the dew-point temperature; condensation starts when the surface temperature falls below it."),
      P("The refrigerating effect per kilogram of refrigerant in a vapour-compression cycle is", "h1 - h4", "Enthalpy drop across evaporator", "RE = h1 (compressor inlet) - h4 (expansion-valve outlet, both at the same enthalpy). Compressor work per kg = h2 - h1."),
      P("R-717 is the designation for", "ammonia refrigerant", "Natural refrigerant", "Ammonia (NH\u2083) is R-717: zero ODP, high COP, excellent heat transfer but toxic and flammable - used in industrial cold stores."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.ice = [
      P("In an ideal Otto cycle the heat addition takes place at", "constant volume", "Spark-ignition cycle", "The Otto cycle adds heat at constant volume between the isentropic compression and expansion strokes, giving pressure-temperature rise at top dead centre."),
      P("In an ideal Diesel cycle the heat addition takes place at", "constant pressure", "Compression-ignition cycle", "The Diesel cycle substitutes constant-pressure heat addition (combustion follows injection at throttled constant pressure) for the constant-volume addition of Otto."),
      P("The compression ratio of a modern petrol engine is typically", "8:1 to 12:1", "SI engine ratio", "Petrol engines run compression ratios of about 8-12 with a spark; diesel (CI) engines range 14-22 and ignite the fuel by the compression heat."),
      P("The knocking tendency in a spark-ignition engine is reduced by using fuel of", "high octane number", "Antiknock rating", "Octane number measures resistance to knock (autoignition at the unburned end-gas). Higher octane fuel allows higher compression without knocking."),
      P("The cetane number of a diesel fuel measures its", "ignition delay (ease of autoignition)", "CI rating", "Cetane number rates the ignition quality - a short ignition delay gives smooth combustion; higher cetane diesel has a lower ignition temperature."),
      P("At the end of the compression stroke in an SI engine, the air-fuel mixture is", "ignited by the spark plug", "Spark ignition", "A spark from the plug ignites the compressed homogeneous mixture at fixed ignition advance; flame propagation burns the charge across the combustion chamber."),
      P("In a diesel engine the fuel is injected", "into hot compressed air, igniting spontaneously", "Compression ignition", "Fuel sprays into air heated above the diesel's autoignition temperature by compression; combustion begins after a short ignition delay without any spark."),
      P("The indicated power of an engine measured from the area of the", "p-v indicator diagram", "Indicator card", "Indicated power comes from the p-v diagram's area (work per cycle) times the number of cycles: IP = (Area x Scale) x N. Brake power is measured on a brake dynamometer."),
      P("The mechanical efficiency of an engine is", "brake power / indicated power", "Power transmission ratio", "Mechanical efficiency \u03B7m = BP/IP; friction power FP = IP - BP. It reflects losses in rubbing, pumping, gears and auxiliaries."),
      P("The heat lost in an engine is greatest to the", "cooling water (or surroundings)", "Energy balance", "Of the fuel energy, about 25-45% goes to useful work, ~30-35% to the cooling system and ~30-35% to exhaust gases and radiation; in practice coolant and exhaust dominate."),
      P("A supercharger is driven by the", "engine crankshaft (mechanically)", "Mechanical boosting", "A supercharger is belt/gear driven from the crankshaft, forcing more air into the cylinders; a turbocharger instead converts exhaust-gas energy in a turbine driving the compressor."),
      P("The main reason for turbocharging a diesel engine is to", "increase the air supply and hence power output", "Boost power density", "Turbocharging compresses the intake air (typical pressure ratio 2-3), raising the air-to-fuel ratio and hence the power output for the same swept volume."),
      P("The two main types of turbocharger compressor are", "radial (centrifugal) and axial", "Turbocompressor classes", "Most engine turbochargers use a centrifugal compressor for its wide stable map; large marine engines may use axial machines feeding the boost volume."),
      P("In a petrol engine a 'lean mixture' has an air-fuel ratio", "greater than the stoichiometric (about 14.7:1)", "Excess air", "Stoichiometric for petrol is ~14.7:1; air-fuel ratios above that are lean (excess air), which improve economy and reduce CO but raise NOx in a narrow lean zone."),
      P("A rich mixture favours for gasoline combustion", "a lower air-fuel ratio to give maximum power", "Power enrichment", "Rich mixtures (AFR below stoichiometric) give slightly higher flame speed and power, but burn incompletely, releasing CO and HC - hence power jets on carburetted engines."),
      P("Detonation (knock) in an SI engine occurs when", "the unburned end-gas autoignites violently", "Abnormal combustion", "Knock is the sudden autoignition of the remaining end-gas ahead of the flame front, ringing the cylinder; it can be controlled by higher octane, less advance and richer mixture."),
      P("Pre-ignition is", "ignition of the charge before the spark by a hot surface", "Hot-spot ignition", "Pre-ignition fires the charge by a glowing valve, carbon or plug tip before the timed spark; it usually degenerates into knock/detonation."),
      P("Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) reduces NOx in engines by", "diluting the intake charge to lower peak combustion temperature", "Thermal dilution", "A portion of cooled exhaust is mixed with intake air, reducing peak flame temperature below the NOx-forming threshold (~1800 K) at some cost in power."),
      P("The function of a catalytic converter in the exhaust is to", "convert CO, HC and NOx into CO\u2082, H\u2082O and N\u2082", "Emission clean-up", "A three-way (oxidation+reduction) catalyst on the exhaust turns pollutants into harmless products; it needs near-stoichiometric mixture and works best when warm."),
      P("A diesel particulate filter (DPF) captures", "soot (particulate matter) from the exhaust", "Soot trapping", "The DPF is a ceramic/honeycomb wall-flow filter trapping soot; it regenerates by burning the collected particles during a warm high-load period."),
      P("The scavenging process in a two-stroke engine", "pushes exhaust gases out with fresh charge", "Charge exchange", "Fresh air/charge entering near the inlet ports pushes the previous exhaust out through the exhaust port - the poorer breathing that limits two-stroke efficiency."),
      P("The two-stroke engine typically shows a disadvantage over four-stroke in", "specific fuel consumption and exhaust emissions", "Breathing penalty", "Port-driven scavenging loses unburned mixture and leaves residual gas, giving poorer SFC and higher HC than the four-stroke for the same power."),
      P("In a four-stroke engine the inlet valve opens", "slightly before TDC (valve overlap) and closes after BDC", "Valve timing", "Inlet opens ~10-30\u00B0 BTDC for better filling and closes ~40-60\u00B0 ABDC using ram effect; exhaust opens ~40\u00B0 BBOC and closes after TDC - overlap keeps scavenging clean."),
      P("Valve overlap refers to the", "period when both inlet and exhaust valves are open", "TDC overlap", "Near TDC both valves open together, using exhaust inertia to draw fresh charge in and helping purge residuals - essential for high-speed breathing."),
      P("Finned cylinder cooling on small engines works by", "increasing the heat-transfer surface to air", "Air cooling", "Cast fins multiply the cylinder's surface area so natural/forced airflow removes combustion heat, keeping piston and valves in their safe temperature range."),
      P("The radiator in a water-cooled engine rejects heat by", "air forced by fan and ram against the finned core", "Coolant-air exchange", "The coolant loop carries heat to the radiator core where engine-driven fans and vehicle ram air cool the finned tubes; a thermostat tracks the temperature."),
      P("An engine's MEP (mean effective pressure) that includes the pumping work is called the", "pumping MEP (PMEP)", "Friction classification", "PMEP is the mean pressure of the pumping loop (gas exchange); net MEP = indicated MEP - PMEP - friction MEP. It appears in the p-v diagram loop."),
      P("The ratio of the maximum combustion pressure to the pressure at the start of compression is the", "pressure ratio \u03C1 (or compression-ignition cut-off)", "Cycle pressure lift", "For a constant-volume (Otto) cycle, the pressure ratio during combustion \u03B1 = p\u2083/p\u2082 measures the heat input; cut-off ratio \u03C1 = v\u2084/v\u2083 governs the Diesel cycle."),
      P("In a gas turbine, the combustion chamber operates at", "essentially constant pressure", "Brayton heat addition", "The combustor adds heat at nearly constant pressure (small loss); turbine gases expand to ambient or low pressure, driving the compressor and load."),
      P("The specific fuel consumption (SFC) of an engine is a measure of", "fuel consumed per unit of power output", "Economy index", "SFC = mf/P (kg/kWh). Brake SFC (BSFC) uses brake power; it is minimised in the mid-load range and increases at idle and at full throttle."),
    ];
SSC_JE_EXTRA5.hydmach = [
      P("The classification of turbines as impulse or reaction depends on whether", "the pressure drops in the nozzle or within the moving blades", "Energy conversion mode", "In an impulse turbine (Pelton) all pressure converts to velocity in the nozzle and the runner gains only kinetic energy; in reaction turbines pressure drops across both fixed and moving blades."),
      P("A Pelton wheel is best suited for", "high heads with low discharges", "High-head plant", "Pelton wheels operate at heads over ~200 m with low flow; the jet from a needle nozzle strikes buckets that split and turn the flow through nearly 180\u00B0."),
      P("The speed ratio of a Pelton wheel is defined as the ratio of", "the bucket (peripheral) velocity to the jet velocity", "u/\u221A(2gH)", "Speed ratio \u03C6 = u/V1 = u/\u221A(2gH). Its optimum value is about 0.46 (0.45-0.5), giving maximum efficiency when the bucket moves at about half the jet speed."),
      P("For maximum hydraulic efficiency of a Pelton wheel, the bucket speed should be", "about half the jet velocity (u = 0.5 V1 for ideal)", "Optimal speed ratio", "Max efficiency occurs near speed ratio 0.5 for the ideal case; with vane angle and friction the common design value is 0.46."),
      P("The number of buckets Z on a Pelton wheel is given approximately by", "Z = (0.5 D/d) + 15", "Empirical bucket count", "For a wheel of pitch diameter D and jet diameter d, empirical bucket number Z = (D/2d) + 15 (a value around 18-25); too few buckets cause spray interference."),
      P("A Francis turbine is a", "reaction machine with inward radial flow", "Mixed-flow reaction", "The Francis turbine runs fully submerged with water entering radially inward and leaving axially; guide vanes regulate flow while pressures are equal over the runner."),
      P("A Kaplan turbine differs from the Francis because the blades", "are adjustable (variable pitch) for part-load efficiency", "Adjustable blades", "Kaplan turbines have adjustable runner blades (and sometimes adjustable guide vanes) so efficiency stays high across a wide range of load and head."),
      P("In a Kaplan turbine the flow is", "axial", "Axial-flow reaction", "Water flows parallel to the shaft through the propeller-type runner, making Kaplan machines the most efficient large flow, low-head reaction turbines (up to ~60 m)."),
      P("The 'draft tube' of a reaction turbine", "recovers kinetic energy at the runner exit as pressure", "Energy recovery", "A diverging draft tube between the runner outlet and the tail race reduces the exit velocity, converting kinetic energy to pressure and making the machine efficient at negative heads."),
      P("The main function of the scroll (spiral) casing of a reaction turbine is to", "distribute water uniformly around the runner", "Even admission", "The spiral casing's decreasing cross-section delivers water at constant velocity around the whole runner periphery, ensuring uniform flow into the guide vanes."),
      P("Cavitation in hydraulic machines is caused by", "local pressure falling below the vapour pressure of water", "Vapour formation", "When local pressure drops below saturation pressure, vapour bubbles form; at recovery they collapse violently, pitting the metal - a common failure of reaction turbines and pumps."),
      P("The Thoma cavitation factor \u03C3 in turbine/pump design relates the", "available NPSH (suction head) to the head of the machine", "NPSH ratio", "Thoma's \u03C3 = NPSH/H. A lower \u03C3 than the critical value means the machine works dangerously close to cavitation and should be raised."),
      P("A centrifugal pump with a forward-curved impeller gives", "a rising head characteristic with higher efficiency at a higher discharge", "Blade angle effect", "Forward-curved blades (outlet angle \u03B2 > 90\u00B0) give high head but a rising/shallow performance curve; backward-curved blades give the flatter, more stable curve used in practice."),
      P("The specific speed Ns of a pump is the speed of a geometrically similar pump that delivers", "unit discharge at unit head (1 m\u00B3/s at 1 m head)", "Similarity number", "Pump specific speed Ns = N\u221A(Q/'H^(3/4)). High Ns characterises high-flow radial-to-axial machines; low Ns the radial impellers of high head."),
      P("The specific speed of a turbine is the speed of a geometrically similar turbine that produces", "unit power under unit head (1 kW at 1 m)", "Turbine classifier", "Turbine Ns = N\u221AP/H^(5/4) (metric). Pelton turbines have the lowest Ns, Francis intermediate, and Kaplan/propeller the highest."),
      P("The effect of the affinity laws says that for constant impeller geometry, discharge varies", "directly with speed (Q \u221D N)", "Centrifugal pump scaling", "Affinity laws: Q \u221D N, H \u221D N\u00B2, P \u221D N\u00B3 at constant impeller diameter; doubling speed gives 4x head and 8x power."),
      P("If the impeller diameter of a pump changes while speed is constant, the discharge and head vary as", "Q \u221D D and H \u221D D\u00B2", "Diameter affinity", "With speed constant, Q \u221D D, H \u221D D\u00B2 and P \u221D D\u00B3; trimming the impeller diameter drops head substantially (a 10% cut removes ~20% head)."),
      P("In a centrifugal pump, the theoretical head developed is given by", "H = (1/g)(Vw2 u2)", "Euler's pump equation", "Euler 's head H = (Vw2u2 - Vw1u1)/g (neglecting pre-whirl Vw1 = 0 for no inlet swirl, H = Vw2u2/g); Vw2 is the tangential whirl component."),
      P("The discharge of a reciprocating pump is", "pulsating (unsteady) because of piston velocity variation", "Pulsation problem", "A single-acting reciprocating pump delivers only during the delivery stroke, at a velocity proportional to the piston speed - hence a pulsating flow needing an air vessel."),
      P("An air vessel fitted to a reciprocating pump", "smooths the pulsations and reduces the inertia head", "Surge damping", "An air vessel close to the pump absorbs the pressure surges, feeding a steadier discharge and lowering the acceleration head required by the long suction pipe."),
      P("The slip of a positive-displacement pump is the difference between", "the theoretical and actual discharge", "Volumetric loss", "Slip = Qth - Qa. Negative slip arises when the actual delivery exceeds the theoretical due to leakage past valves adding to the out-of-step flow."),
      P("The indicator diagram of a reciprocating pump shows", "the pressure in the cylinder during the stroke", "Pump cycle work", "The indicator diagram shows suction and delivery pressure lines; its area times the crank rate gives the indicated power and reveals losses from inertia and friction."),
      P("In a water-turbine test, the power delivered from the shaft is called", "brake power (measured by brake dynamometer)", "Shaft output", "Brake power = 2\u03C0NT measured at the pulley-brake; hydraulic power = \u03C1gQH; overall efficiency = BP/\u03C1gQH."),
      P("Specific speed is used to choose a turbine because different machines of the same Ns are", "geometrically similar and behave identically at the same load", "Dynamic similarity", "Turbines of equal specific speed are geometrically and dynamically similar, so their performance curves coincide; Ns therefore selects the Pelton/Francis/Kaplan type for the site."),
      P("The discharge coefficient in a Pelton nozzle measures", "the actual jet diameter ratio to the nozzle bore", "Cd = d_jet/d_nozzle", "Cd for a Pelton nozzle relates the actual jet diameter to the nozzle: d_jet = Cd d_nozzle (about 0.98-0.995), governing the jet velocity from the head."),
      P("In a hydraulic ram the energy source is", "the kinetic/momentum energy of a falling water column", "Water hammer lift", "A ram converts the momentum of a large flow of water falling through a small head into a small high-head lift by water-hammer action - pumping water without an external prime mover."),
      P("A hydraulic couple driving a capstan from a pump is an example of a", "rotary actuator", "Hydraulic rotary drive", "The oil under pressure drives a rotary actuator (vane or piston motor) converting pressure energy to shaft torque; flow controls speed, pressure controls torque."),
      P("The main advantage of using hydraulic (hydrostatic) drives in heavy machinery is their", "high power-to-weight ratio and effortless speed/force control", "Hydrostatic benefits", "Hydrostatic transmissions give infinitely variable speed with a high torque/power density, smooth reversing and overload protection by cracking relief valves."),
      P("NPSH available is defined as the", "net positive suction head at the pump inlet above vapour pressure", "Pump suction margin", "NPSHa = (ps + \u03C1ghs - pv)/\u03C1g; it must exceed NPSHr for the pump so the inlet pressure never falls to vapour pressure, avoiding cavitation."),
      P("In a hydraulic turbine governing system the governor adjusts", "the guide-vane opening (or nozzle opening) to meet load", "Speed control", "The governor senses speed and moves the spear/needle (Pelton) or guide vanes (Francis/Kaplan) to alter the flow, holding steady speed against changing load."),
    ];
  if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = SSC_JE_EXTRA5;
        if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.SSC_JE_EXTRA5 = SSC_JE_EXTRA5;
      })();